window.__ARCHAIC_MD__ = "# THE ARCHAIC ERA (III century BC -- beginning of I century BC)\n\nRoman literature does not begin with a moment of spontaneous inspiration. It begins with a practical problem: Rome has conquered the Greek world, or most of it, and now has to figure out what to do with all this Greek culture. The answer, for the first two centuries of Latin literature, is essentially: translate it, adapt it, and quietly pretend it was your idea all along.\n\nThe earliest Latin authors are almost entirely occupied with importing Greek literary forms into Latin. Tragedy, comedy, epic -- all arrive in Rome via Greece, carried by writers who were often themselves Greek or bilingual. The native Italian tradition, most notably the Saturnian meter and the improvised *fescennine* verses, survives in fragments or in occasional use, but it does not set the tone. Greek models do. This is not a sign of cultural weakness: it is a deliberate act of literary construction, the creation of a literature from scratch by a civilization that had the ambition to match Greece and the pragmatic intelligence to borrow what it needed.\n\nStylistically, the Archaic Era is defined by features that classical authors would later view as rough or unpolished: archaic morphology, pre-classical spelling and vocabulary, the Saturnian meter (whose exact rules remain debated to this day), and a directness of expression that has more in common with oral poetry than with the studied elegance of the Augustan age. This is not a defect -- it is the texture of a literature still finding its feet. Reading these authors means reading Latin before it became \"Latin\" in the classical sense. The language is recognizable but foreign in ways that go beyond mere vocabulary.\n\nBy the middle of the era the picture shifts. With Plautus and Terence, Roman comedy establishes a fully Latin voice -- colloquial, energetic, theatrically sophisticated -- even while drawing on Greek originals. With Cato, Latin prose finds its first great practitioner, and for the first time a major author is not adapting Greek material but writing about Rome directly, in a Latin he consciously shapes as a political and cultural instrument. With Lucilius, Latin satire is invented -- a genre the Romans would later claim as entirely their own.\n\nPractically speaking, the surviving corpus of the Archaic Era is almost entirely fragmentary, with two major exceptions: the comedies of Plautus and Terence, and the prose of Cato the Elder. For most other authors in this era, we have at best scattered lines preserved by later grammarians who quoted them precisely because they were already archaic and strange. This means that most difficulty ratings for this era carry an asterisk, and NC (Not Comparable) is a frequent and honest outcome.\n\n---\n\n# LIVIUS ANDRONICUS (c. 284-204 BC)\n\n\n![Livius Andronicus](./images/livius_andronicus.jpg)\n*Livius Andronicus *(imaginary reconstruction)**\n## Biography\n\nLivius Andronicus is the founding father of Latin literature -- practically the Big Bang of Roman letters. Born around 284 BC in Tarentum (modern Taranto), a Greek colony in southern Italy, he was brought to Rome as a slave after the Roman conquest of the city in 272 BC. Acquired by the Livii family, he took the name Livius Andronicus and worked as a tutor. He was eventually freed, likely because of his exceptional intellectual abilities.\n\nIn 240 BC he did something remarkable: he staged the first theatrical performance in Latin at Rome, a comedy and a tragedy, both adapted from Greek originals. That date is conventionally treated as the birth of Latin literature. He founded an entire literary tradition. As a former slave.\n\nAs if that were not enough, in 207 BC -- with the Second Punic War still raging and Hannibal still in Italy -- the Senate commissioned him to compose a propitiatory hymn to Juno to appease the gods. The hymn worked well enough that the guild of writers and actors (*collegium scribarum histrionumque*) received official recognition from the Roman state, partly in his honor. Not bad for a Greek ex-slave.\n\n## Main Works\n\n- **Odusia** -- Latin translation of Homer's Odyssey in Saturnian meter. Only fragments survive, but it served as a school text in Rome for generations. Cicero recalled studying it as a boy -- not with particular enthusiasm.\n- **Tragedies** (*Achilles*, *Ajax Mastigophorus*, *Andromeda*, *Equos Troianus*, *Tereus*, among others) -- adaptations from Greek models. All lost; only scattered lines survive.\n- **Comedies** -- adaptations from Greek models. Almost entirely lost.\n\n## Style and Difficulty\n\nLivius Andronicus writes in archaic Latin. If you come to his text expecting the classical clarity of Caesar, you are in for a surprise. Words like *insece* (for *narra*), *topper* (for *statim*), *dacrimas* (for *lacrimas*), and *nequinont* (for *nequeunt*) are the norm, not the exception. The text is not obscure by stylistic choice -- it is archaic by historical necessity. Livius is writing before literary Latin exists as a standard. He is essentially inventing the rules as he goes.\n\nThe Odusia uses the **Saturnian meter**, the native Italic verse form. The problem is that nobody today -- and already few people in antiquity -- fully agrees on how this meter works. Horace already found it rough (*\"horridus ille / defluxit numerus Saturnius\"*). Using it as a reading aid is like navigating with a medieval map.\n\nThe main sources of difficulty are: archaic lexicon with pre-classical morphology; irregular Saturnian meter that cannot be used to guide comprehension the way classical meters can; the fragmentary nature of the corpus (surviving lines are isolated from their context); and Greek-influenced constructions, since the text is a direct translation of Homer.\n\n## Latin Excerpt, Translation and Analysis\n\n**Latin text** *(The Latin Library, thelatinlibrary.com)*\n\n> Virum mihi, Camena, insece versutum\n> *(Odusia I, 1 -- rendering of Odyssey 1.1)*\n\n> Igitur demum Ulixi cor frixit prae pavore.\n> *(Odusia V, 297 -- rendering of Odyssey 5.297)*\n\n**Italian translation** *(original)*\n\n> Raccontami, o Camena, dell'uomo dall'ingegno molteplice.\n\n> E allora, finalmente, il cuore di Ulisse si gelò di terrore.\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> Tell me, O Camena, of the man of manifold cunning.\n\n> And then at last the heart of Ulysses froze with dread.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThe first line is the heart of the Odusia: Livius's rendering of Homer's immortal opening (*\"Ἄνδρα μοι ἔννεπε, Μοῦσα, πολύτροπον\"*). Every word repays attention.\n\n*Virum* translates *ἄνδρα*, though in Latin the emphatic force of Homer's opening is slightly diluted -- in Greek, \"the man\" is the absolute first word of the poem, thrown at the reader immediately. *Camena* replaces the Greek Muse: the Camenae were Italic water divinities, and the substitution is a deliberate act of cultural translation, not a failure of imagination. *Insece* is an archaic imperative form of *insequor* (narrate, follow); it was already obscure in the classical period. *Versutum* renders *πολύτροπον* (\"of many turns, of many wiles\"): a happy choice, since *versutus* carries exactly the right connotation of slippery, fox-like intelligence.\n\nThe second line corresponds to the moment when Odysseus, alone at sea, sees the whirlpool of Charybdis open before him. *Cor frixit* (\"his heart froze\") is a vivid archaic expression -- *frigesco* applied to the heart to indicate terror. The construction *prae pavore* (cause expressed with *prae* + ablative) is fully classical syntax; one of the moments where Livius anticipates rather than lags behind the norm.\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: NC (Not Comparable)**\n\nThe intrinsic per-line difficulty of the Odusia would place Livius solidly in Tier A: archaic lexicon, pre-classical morphology, and a metrically opaque verse form all converge. However, the surviving corpus is far too sparse for a reliable rating. No complete work survives. The fragments are isolated lines preserved by grammarians who quoted them precisely because they were already strange and old -- roughly 80 verses in total. Applying a full difficulty rating to this material would be like rating a mountain by the color of three pebbles found at its base. NC is the honest call. If pressed, Tier B would be the scaled-down equivalent.\n\n---\n\n# GNAEUS NAEVIUS (c. 270-201 BC)\n\n\n![Gnaeus Naevius](./images/naevius.jpg)\n*Gnaeus Naevius *(imaginary reconstruction)**\n## Biography\n\nIf Livius Andronicus is the founding father of Latin literature, Naevius is its first troublemaker -- and the first Latin author we know of who was actually Roman. Born around 270 BC, probably in Campania, he fought in the First Punic War as a Roman citizen and soldier. This is not a detail: Naevius was the first Latin poet who could write about Rome's wars because he had been in them.\n\nHe worked primarily as a playwright, producing both tragedies adapted from Greek models and comedies that veered increasingly toward topical political satire. This second tendency got him into serious trouble. Naevius had a gift for the cutting remark, and he used it freely against Rome's most powerful families. His most famous provocation was a single line aimed at the Metelli: *\"Fato Metelli Romae fiunt consules\"* -- \"The Metelli become consuls at Rome by fate.\" The word *fato* carries a double edge: fate, yes, but also stupidity (from *fatuus*). The Metelli responded with equal economy: *\"Dabunt malum Metelli Naevio poetae\"* -- \"The Metelli will give the poet Naevius what he deserves.\" They were as good as their word. Naevius was imprisoned, and eventually exiled to Utica in North Africa, where he died around 201 BC.\n\nHe also invented a genre: the *fabula praetexta*, a tragedy built around Roman historical or legendary subjects rather than Greek ones. Rome writing about Rome, on a stage. That was new.\n\n## Main Works\n\n- **Bellum Poenicum** -- a historical epic on the First Punic War, written in Saturnian meter. The only Latin epic before Ennius's Annales. Survives in about 67 fragments; later divided into seven books by the grammarian Octavius Lampadio.\n- **Comedies** -- a substantial output, some titles survive (*Acontizomenos*, *Ariolus*, *Carbonaria*, among others). Almost entirely lost.\n- **Tragedies** -- including *fabula praetexta* works (*Romulus*, *Clastidium*). Lost.\n\n## Style and Difficulty\n\nNaevius writes in a Latin that is archaic but already slightly more confident than Livius Andronicus. His vocabulary still features pre-classical forms and constructions, but there is a directness to his expression -- a soldier's bluntness -- that is oddly readable once you adjust to the archaic surface. Where Livius can feel like pure translationese from Homer, Naevius feels like a Roman writing about Roman things in a Latin that is still figuring itself out.\n\nThe *Bellum Poenicum* uses the Saturnian meter, with all the usual difficulty that brings. The epitaph (fragment 67), however, is metrically cleaner and serves as a useful introduction to his voice. The comedies, judging by their titles and the few surviving lines, show a more colloquial register -- faster, looser, closer to spoken Latin.\n\nThe main difficulties are the same as for Livius: archaic lexicon, Saturnian meter, and fragmentary context. Naevius adds one complication of his own: his political and satirical content requires familiarity with the Roman public figures and events of the mid-third century BC, many of whom are obscure even to specialists.\n\n## Latin Excerpts, Translations and Analyses\n\n### Bellum Poenicum -- Book I, Fragment 8\n\n**Latin text** *(The Latin Library, thelatinlibrary.com)*\n\n> amborum uxores\n> noctu Troiad exibant capitibus opertis,\n> flentes ambae, abeuntes lacrimis cum multis.\n\n*(Bellum Poenicum, Liber I, fragment 8)*\n\n**Italian translation** *(original)*\n\n> Le mogli di entrambi\n> uscivano di notte da Troia col capo velato,\n> piangendo entrambe, partendo tra molte lacrime.\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> The wives of both\n> went out from Troy at night with covered heads,\n> both weeping, departing with many tears.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThis fragment comes from Book I of the *Bellum Poenicum*, where Naevius connected the Punic War narrative back to the fall of Troy and the departure of Aeneas -- an innovation that shaped all subsequent Roman epic, and that Virgil would develop into the *Aeneid*. The subject of *amborum* (of both) is disputed: most scholars read it as the wives of Anchises and Aeneas, departing together from the burning city.\n\nWhat is striking is how much Naevius achieves in three short lines. The midnight departure (*noctu*), the covered heads (*capitibus opertis* -- a Roman mourning gesture any contemporary audience would recognize), and the careful doubling of *amborum* / *ambae* (both/both) across the passage create a quietly devastating effect. This is not the heroic register of departure: it is the register of grief and displacement, and it belongs to the women left to walk out while the men steer the ships. It is also the most cinematic moment in archaic Latin epic.\n\nMetrically, this is Saturnian verse. The archaic form *Troiad* (from Troy -- an old ablative construction) and the adverb *noctu* (at night) are characteristic of the register; *capitibus opertis* is an ablative absolute. The syntax is clean and paratactic: three images in sequence, no subordination. This is Naevius at his most direct, and his most human.\n\n---\n\n### Epitaphium Naevii -- Fragment 67\n\n**Latin text** *(The Latin Library, thelatinlibrary.com)*\n\n> immortales mortales si foret fas flere,\n> flerent divae Camenae Naevium poetam.\n> itaque postquam est Orcho traditus thesauro,\n> obliti sunt Romae loquier lingua Latina.\n\n*(Epitaphium Naevii -- fragment 67)*\n\n**Italian translation** *(original)*\n\n> Se fosse lecito agli immortali piangere i mortali,\n> le divine Camenae piangerebbero il poeta Nevio.\n> Dacché fu consegnato al tesoro di Orco,\n> a Roma hanno dimenticato di parlare in lingua latina.\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> If it were right for immortals to weep for mortals,\n> the divine Camenae would weep for the poet Naevius.\n> And so, since he was handed over to the treasury of Orcus,\n> Rome has forgotten how to speak the Latin tongue.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThis epitaph -- either written by Naevius himself (which would be entirely in character) or by admirers shortly after his death -- is one of the most striking self-assessments in all of Latin literature. The arrogance is magnificent and entirely deliberate.\n\nThe opening conditional, *\"si foret fas\"*, sets up a reversal of the cosmic order: it is not the mortals who mourn, but the immortals. The *Camenae* -- the same Roman divinities Livius used to translate Homer's Muse -- weep for a poet, elevating him to the status of a loss felt by the divine. *Orcho traditus thesauro* is the striking expression for death: Orcus (the underworld) treated as a treasure vault that has received a deposit. The word *thesauro* implies that Naevius was a treasure, not a casualty. Then the closing blow: since his death, Rome has forgotten how to speak Latin. Not just how to write it -- how to speak it.\n\nMetrically, this is Saturnian verse, but relatively regular by the standards of the meter. The four lines show characteristic features: the heavy end-weight of each line, the slightly irregular stress pattern, and the paratactic flow (conjunctions do the work, subordination is minimal). *Loquier* is the archaic infinitive form for *loqui*, a useful reminder that even in this relatively polished piece the archaic surface is always present.\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: B -- Difficult**\n\nNaevius has more surviving material than Livius Andronicus -- roughly 67 fragments of the *Bellum Poenicum* plus comedy titles and a handful of other lines -- enough to attempt a rating, if cautiously. The intrinsic per-line difficulty would place him at Tier A: archaic lexicon, Saturnian meter, and content that presupposes knowledge of mid-third century Roman politics. Scaled down one step for the fragmentary corpus, Tier B is the appropriate call. The epitaph above, being metrically cleaner and self-contained, is actually closer to Tier C in isolation -- which is part of why it makes a good entry point.\n\n---\n\n# QUINTUS ENNIUS (239-169 BC)\n\n\n![Quintus Ennius](./images/ennius.jpg)\n*Quintus Ennius *(imaginary reconstruction)**\n## Biography\n\nQuintus Ennius is the most important Latin author nobody reads anymore. In antiquity he was the great poet: Lucretius called him \"our Ennius\"; Virgil quietly borrowed from him on almost every page of the Aeneid; Cicero quoted him constantly and from memory, for pleasure. Today he survives in about six hundred lines of fragmentary epic and scattered other pieces, and most students encounter him only as a footnote. This is a shame.\n\nBorn in 239 BC in Rudiae, a small town in Calabria at the crossroads of Latin, Greek, and Oscan cultures, Ennius was trilingual from birth. He famously claimed to have \"three hearts\" (*tria corda*) because he could think and speak in three languages -- which, depending on how you look at it, is either the most cosmopolitan thing a Roman ever said, or an early and very Latin form of showing off. He served as a centurion during the Second Punic War and was brought to Rome around 204 BC by none other than Cato the Elder -- which tells you something about Cato, and rather more about Ennius. Once in Rome he became a close friend of Scipio Africanus, moving in the most powerful circles of the Republic while living modestly on the Aventine hill. He died in 169 BC at seventy, reportedly from gout. For a poet of his standing, a disappointingly undramatic end.\n\nHis single greatest contribution is metrical: he replaced the Saturnian meter with the Greek dactylic hexameter for Latin epic. This was not merely a technical choice. It was a cultural declaration -- that Latin poetry could compete with Homer on Homer's own terms.\n\n## Main Works\n\n- **Annales** -- an epic in 18 books covering Roman history from the fall of Troy to Ennius's own time. The foundational monument of Archaic Latin poetry. About 600 lines survive, preserved mainly by later grammarians. [Fragmentary]\n- **Tragedies** -- adaptations from Greek, primarily Euripides (*Medea*, *Thyestes*, *Iphigenia*, among others). All lost except fragments.\n- **Saturae** -- a miscellaneous collection, the earliest Latin work to carry the name *satura*. Lost.\n- **Epicharmus** and **Euhemerus** -- philosophical works in prose and verse. Lost.\n\n## Style and Difficulty\n\nEnnius writes at a crossroads. He is archaic by our standards -- his Latin still has forms like *induperator* (for *imperator*), *ollis* (for *illis*), and *postque magisque* -- but for Romans of the classical period he already represented a kind of founding grandeur. The dactylic hexameter he introduced makes him considerably more accessible than Livius or Naevius for one simple reason: the meter is learnable and regular enough to assist comprehension. Once you can read hexameter, you have a framework. You do not have that with Saturnian verse.\n\nThat said, archaic vocabulary remains the core difficulty, with morphological forms that disappeared before Cicero was born. The syntax is largely paratactic -- events placed side by side rather than subordinated -- which creates a staccato energy but is not, in itself, syntactically intricate. What is complex is allusion density: the Annales is saturated with Homeric echoes, epic conventions, and references to Roman historical figures that require constant contextual knowledge.\n\n## Latin Excerpt, Translation and Analysis\n\n**Latin text** *(The Latin Library, www.thelatinlibrary.com)*\n\n> Unus homo nobis cunctando restituit rem.\n> Non enim rumores ponebat ante salutem.\n> Ergo postque magisque viri nunc gloria claret.\n>\n> *(Annales IX -- on Fabius Maximus Cunctator)*\n\n> Nos sumus Romani qui fuimus ante Rudini.\n>\n> *(Annales XVIII -- de se ipso)*\n\n**Italian translation** *(original)*\n\n> Un solo uomo, con il suo indugiare, ci ha restituito la patria.\n> Non anteponeva le voci di popolo alla salvezza.\n> E così la gloria di quell'uomo ora splende sempre di più.\n\n> Noi siamo Romani, noi che fummo un tempo di Rudiae.\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> One man by his delaying restored our fortunes.\n> He did not put rumor before the safety of the state.\n> And so, more and more, that man's glory shines.\n\n> We are Romans now, we who were once men of Rudiae.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThe three-line passage on Fabius Maximus -- the dictator who famously refused open battle with Hannibal during the Second Punic War, earning the nickname *Cunctator* (the Delayer) and the contempt of the Roman public until his strategy was eventually proved correct -- is one of the most quoted fragments of Latin literature. Its power lies in compression.\n\n*Unus homo*: one single man, emphasis on the individual against the mass. *Cunctando*: the ablative gerund embeds the strategy itself into the syntax. *Restituit rem*: three words that contain the entire achievement -- *res* is not just \"the situation\" but the Roman state, Roman reality, the thing that matters. The second line wraps a moral judgment in an antithesis: *rumores* (popular noise) against *salutem* (survival). In the third line *postque magisque* -- an archaic double construction for \"more and more afterward\" -- gives historical validation its cumulative rhythm.\n\nThe single autobiographical line *de se ipso* rewards attention: \"We are Romans now, we who were once men of Rudiae.\" Ennius writes it near the end of his life, looking back at his journey from a Calabrian town where nobody had heard of Latin poetry. He has spent his career making Rome great in verse. He has also, in doing so, become Roman. The line says both things at once.\n\nMetrically, these are the first lines in this document where the dactylic hexameter can be scanned with reasonable confidence. Notice the spondaic weight in *Unus homo nobis cunctando restituit rem*: the rhythm itself delays.\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: B (borderline A) -- Difficult**\n\nEnnius has more surviving material than any other author in the Archaic Era -- about 600 lines of the Annales plus dramatic fragments -- enough for a confident rating. Per-line difficulty is high: archaic vocabulary and morphology, epic diction, dense historical allusion. Without the fragmentary adjustment this would be a firm A. With it, Tier B stands as the overall rating, but the hardest passages -- the battle scenes, the dream sequences in Book I -- genuinely touch A. Think of it as high B / low A: the tier reflects the average reading experience, not the ceiling.\n\n---\n\n# TITUS MACCIUS PLAUTUS (c. 254-184 BC)\n\n\n![Titus Maccius Plautus](./images/plautus.jpg)\n*Titus Maccius Plautus *(imaginary reconstruction)**\n## Biography\n\nEven the name is a joke. \"Titus Maccius Plautus\" is almost certainly not the name this man was born with. *Maccius* is the Latinized form of Maccus, the stock clown of the Atellan farce -- the slapstick theatrical tradition from Atella in Campania, where a dim-witted buffoon named Maccus was roughly the Roman equivalent of Punchinello. *Plautus* means \"flat-footed\" or possibly \"floppy-eared,\" both animal-derived insults that double as theatrical nicknames. So what we have is: Titus the Clown, the Flat-Footed. Either this is a stage name he adopted deliberately, or his parents had an unusual approach to child-rearing. Scholars lean toward the former.\n\nWhat we know about his life comes almost entirely from a single paragraph in Aulus Gellius's *Noctes Atticae* (III.3), written more than three centuries after Plautus's death, and citing the scholar Varro as the source. According to this tradition, Plautus was born around 254 BC in Sarsina, a hill town in Umbria. He came to Rome and made money in some theatrical capacity -- acting, possibly, or carpentry, or building stage sets -- then lost everything in overseas trading ventures and ended up working in a grain mill, grinding by hand, the lowest form of labor available to a free man. There, between flour sacks, he allegedly wrote his first three plays. The story has the satisfying shape of a fable rather than biography, and may be approximately true.\n\nHe went on to become the most popular playwright in Roman history. In the second century BC, the scholar Varro sifted through the 130 plays attributed to him and identified 21 as certainly authentic -- and those 21 are precisely the ones that survive, making Plautus, alongside Terence, the best-preserved dramatist of the Roman Republic. He died around 184 BC, almost exactly when Cato the Elder was at the height of his powers -- a nice collision of Rome's two very different voices.\n\nPlautus's comedies are all *fabulae palliatae*: Greek-costumed comedies, adaptations of Greek New Comedy (primarily Menander and Diphilus). He takes Greek plots, Greek character types -- the clever slave, the braggart soldier, the young man in love, the miserly father, the pimp -- and translates them not just into Latin but into something distinctly Roman and distinctly loud. Where his Greek sources were relatively restrained, Plautus is exuberant: more puns, more physical slapstick, more wordplay, more improvisation-style asides, more songs. The result is not a translation. It is a transformation.\n\nA small biographical postscript that says everything about the man: Plautus reportedly requested that *Pseudolus* be performed at his funeral. He spent his life writing jokes and then, at the end, asked for more jokes. There is a kind of dignity in that.\n\n## Main Works\n\n- **Menaechmi** -- twin brothers, mistaken identity, chaos. Shakespeare's *Comedy of Errors* is based on this. [Complete]\n- **Amphitryon** -- Jupiter impersonates a mortal husband; Mercury disguises himself as the slave Sosia. The only surviving Roman comedy on a mythological subject, and the first text to use the word *tragicomoedia*. [Complete]\n- **Miles Gloriosus** -- the braggart soldier, one of the most copied characters in Western comedy. [Complete]\n- **Pseudolus** -- Plautus's own favorite; the clever slave outsmarts a pimp, a soldier, and his own master in one afternoon. Reportedly performed at Plautus's funeral. [Complete]\n- **Aulularia** -- the miser and his pot of gold. Model for Moliere's *L'Avare*. [Complete, with lacuna at the end]\n\nTwenty-one plays survive in total.\n\n## Style and Difficulty\n\nHere is the trap Plautus sets for the unwary student: he looks easy. The vocabulary is everyday, the syntax is paratactic, the characters speak rapidly in short bursts. No dense philosophy, no Ciceronian periods, no archaic epic register. Plautus, apparently, is just people talking.\n\nHe is not just people talking. He is the most linguistically demanding author in the Archaic Era, and a serious contender for the hardest author in all of Latin literature.\n\nThe core difficulty is the *sermo cotidianus* -- the everyday speech register. Classical Latin as taught in schools is the formal register of the first century BC: Cicero, Caesar, Livy. Plautus wrote 150 years before that register existed. His Latin is colloquial, rapid, theatrical, and full of elements that appear nowhere else in surviving Latin simply because nobody else bothered to write down how people actually talked. When Pseudolus calls a love letter unreadable because *gallina scripsit* -- a chicken wrote it -- that is a Roman street joke, and unpacking it requires cultural fluency that no dictionary entry provides. Some Plautine jokes are no longer fully recoverable; the context that made them land has been lost.\n\nArchaic morphology adds another layer. Forms like *Sibulla* (for *Sibylla*), *pellegam* (for *perlegam*), *opsecro* (for *obsecro*) appear constantly -- the student trained on Caesar will find a strange form on almost every line. On top of this sits an enormous colloquial vocabulary: slang, diminutives, Plautine coinages, Greek loanwords used for punchlines, obscenity euphemisms. These are in the footnotes of the dictionary, not the main entries. **Lexicon: HIGH.**\n\nThe wordplay is structural, not decorative. The puns are the architecture. Missing one can break comprehension of the next three lines, and the joke density is relentless -- four different registers of humor can appear in fourteen consecutive lines. **Stylistic figures: HIGH.**\n\nMeter is more varied than it looks. The iambic senarii of spoken dialogue are learnable and actually help comprehension once mastered; but Plautus shifts to trochaic septenarii for emotional scenes and into lyric *cantica* -- cretic and bacchiac rhythms -- for musical numbers. The *cantica* are among the hardest passages in Latin verse. Syntax, paradoxically, is the one forgiving element: rapid parataxis and ellipsis, but not Tacitean compression. **Medium: MEDIUM-HIGH; syntax: MEDIUM; allusion density: LOW-MEDIUM** (the Greek originals lurk behind each play, but the dialogue itself carries little unmarked mythological allusion).\n\nThe *sermo cotidianus* constitutes its own genre burden: it demands cultural immersion that grammar study alone cannot supply. **Genre burden: HIGH.** Three criteria at HIGH -- lexicon, figures, genre -- clears the technical threshold for Tier S, which requires more than two criteria at maximum difficulty simultaneously. The reason for stopping at high A is that syntactic complexity and allusion density stay at medium: S tier should feel like total resistance on every front. Plautus is brutal on specific dimensions but offers footholds. Individual *cantica* scenes may touch S in isolation.\n\n## Latin Excerpt, Translation and Analysis\n\n**Latin text** *(The Latin Library, www.thelatinlibrary.com)*\n\n> PSEVDOLVS: Mos tibi geretur. sed quid hoc, quaeso?\n> CALIDORVS: Quid est?\n> PS: Vt opinor, quaerunt litterae hae sibi liberos:\n> alia aliam scandit.\n> CAL: Ludis iam ludo tuo?\n> PS: Has quidem pol credo nisi Sibulla legerit,\n> interpretari alium posse neminem.\n> CAL: Cur inclementer dicis lepidis litteris\n> lepidis tabellis lepida conscriptis manu?\n> PS: An, opsecro hercle, habent quas gallinae manus?\n> nam has quidem gallina scripsit.\n> CAL: Odiosus mihi es.\n> lege vel tabellas redde.\n> PS: Immo enim pellegam.\n> advortito animum.\n> CAL: Non adest.\n> PS: At tu cita.\n> CAL: Immo ego tacebo, tu istinc ex cera cita;\n> nam istic meus animus nunc est, non in pectore.\n> PS: Tuam amicam video, Calidore.\n> CAL: Vbi ea est, opsecro?\n> PS: Eccam in tabellis porrectam: in cera cubat.\n>\n> *(Pseudolus I.i, vv. 22-36)*\n\n**Italian translation** *(original)*\n\n> PSEUDOLO: Come vuoi. Ma cos'e' questo, di grazia?\n> CALIDORO: Cosa c'e'?\n> PS: A mio parere, queste lettere cercano dei figli:\n> l'una si arrampica sull'altra.\n> CAL: Gia' a fare il tuo solito gioco?\n> PS: Per Polluce, ci credo: se non e' la Sibilla a leggerle,\n> nessun altro ci riuscirebbe.\n> CAL: Come puoi parlare cosi' male di belle lettere,\n> scritte su belle tavolette da una bella mano?\n> PS: Ma, per Ercole, le galline hanno forse le mani?\n> Perche' questa l'ha scritta una gallina.\n> CAL: Sei insopportabile.\n> Leggile o ridammele.\n> PS: No, no, le leggo fino in fondo.\n> Fa' attenzione.\n> CAL: Non ci sono.\n> PS: Allora sforzati.\n> CAL: No, sto' zitto io; tirala fuori tu dalla cera;\n> perche' il mio animo e' la', non nel petto.\n> PS: Vedo la tua amica, Calidoro.\n> CAL: Dov'e'? Ti prego.\n> PS: Eccola, distesa sulle tavolette: giace nella cera.\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> PSEUDOLUS: As you wish. But what in the world is this?\n> CALIDORUS: What is it?\n> PS: As I see it, these letters are looking to have children:\n> one is climbing on top of another.\n> CAL: Starting with your usual games already?\n> PS: By Pollux, I truly believe that unless the Sibyl were to read them,\n> nobody else could make any sense of them.\n> CAL: How can you speak so harshly of beautiful letters\n> written on a beautiful tablet in a beautiful hand?\n> PS: By Hercules, do chickens have hands?\n> Because a chicken wrote this.\n> CAL: You are insufferable.\n> Read it or give it back.\n> PS: No, no, I will read it all the way through.\n> Pay attention.\n> CAL: I am not here.\n> PS: Then make an effort.\n> CAL: No, I will be quiet; you call her out of the wax;\n> because my soul is there right now, not in my chest.\n> PS: I can see your girlfriend, Calidorus.\n> CAL: Where is she? Please.\n> PS: There she is, stretched out on the tablet: she is lying in the wax.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThis passage is from the opening scene of *Pseudolus* -- the first extended exchange between the clever slave Pseudolus and his lovesick young master Calidorus. Calidorus has been walking around for days clutching a wax tablet (a letter from his beloved Phoenicium, sold by a pimp to a soldier) and weeping over it. Pseudolus finally takes the tablet and attempts to read it.\n\nMetrically, these lines are iambic senarii -- the dialogue register of Roman comedy, relatively approachable by the standards of Latin verse. The meter here does not fight the reader. Everything else does.\n\nThe first joke operates on two levels simultaneously. *Quaerunt litterae hae sibi liberos: alia aliam scandit* -- \"these letters are looking to have children: one is climbing on top of another.\" The surface meaning is visual: the handwriting is so cramped and tangled that individual letters appear to be piled on top of each other. But *scandere* is the technical verb for scanning verse -- \"to climb\" a meter. Plautus is making a self-referential joke about his own written text. The letters of the love letter cannot be scanned; the letters of the play you are watching can. Whether this is deliberate or incidental is, with Plautus, the wrong question.\n\n*Sibulla* is the archaic spelling of *Sibylla*, the prophetess of Cumae. Pseudolus is comparing illegible handwriting to prophetic inscription -- the Sibyl's oracles were famously written on leaves, scattered by the wind, and notoriously difficult to reassemble. It is also a joke about the grandiosity of calling in divine interpreters for a teenage love letter.\n\nThe gallina joke -- *An, opsecro hercle, habent quas gallinae manus?* -- is structured as a rhetorical question followed by a concluding blow: *nam has quidem gallina scripsit*. The oath *opsecro hercle* (\"by Hercules, I beg you\") is pure Plautine verbal tic: *opsecro* (archaic for *obsecro*) and *hercle* appear dozens of times across the comedies as conversational filler and emphasis markers. They are the Roman equivalent of \"I mean, come on.\"\n\n*Pellegam* (for *perlegam*, \"I will read through\") and *advortito* (for *advertito*, \"pay attention\") are further archaic forms, unremarkable in Plautus but invisible in school Latin.\n\nThe emotional high point of the exchange is *nam istic meus animus nunc est, non in pectore*: \"my soul is there right now, not in my chest.\" Calidorus is saying that his whole being has followed Phoenicium into the wax tablet she wrote. It is a genuinely touching line -- a real moment of feeling in a comic scene. Pseudolus immediately punctures it: *Eccam in tabellis porrectam: in cera cubat* -- \"there she is, stretched out on the tablet: she is lying in the wax.\" *Porrectam* (from *porrigo*, \"to stretch out\") carries the additional connotation of a corpse laid out for viewing. Pseudolus is being simultaneously affectionate and brutal, which is his entire character in a single line.\n\nNote what is happening structurally: four different registers of humor -- physical (the chicken), intellectual (the Sibyl), metatextual (scanning verse), and emotional (the soul in the wax) -- appear in fourteen lines of dialogue. This density is not unusual for Plautus. It is the baseline.\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: A (high) -- Very Difficult. Borderline S.**\n\nBrutal on lexicon, wordplay, and colloquial register; partially offset by clear syntax and low allusion density. The *sermo cotidianus* makes Plautus uniquely hard in a way the tier scale does not fully capture: not uniformly opaque, but requiring cultural fluency that cannot be studied into. *Cantica* scenes in isolation may touch S.\n\n---\n\n# MARCUS PORCIUS CATO -- CATO THE ELDER (234-149 BC)\n\n\n![Marcus Porcius Cato](./images/cato.jpg)\n*Marcus Porcius Cato*\n## Biography\n\nMarcus Porcius Cato is the Roman whom every Roman tried to imitate and nobody actually wanted to be. Austere, combative, obsessively frugal, deeply suspicious of anything that smelled of Greece -- he was the self-appointed conscience of the Republic, and he never stopped reminding you of this.\n\nBorn in 234 BC in Tusculum, a hill town in the hills outside Rome, Cato was a man of equestrian origins who built his career through military service and sheer force of personality rather than aristocratic connections. He fought under Fabius Maximus during the Second Punic War -- yes, the same Fabius Maximus whose delaying strategy Ennius had just immortalized. The war made him, and he never stopped thinking of himself as a soldier first.\n\nHere is the contradiction at the heart of Cato: he is the man who brought Ennius to Rome from Sardinia in 204 BC. Let that sink in. The most ferociously anti-Greek voice in Roman public life personally imported the poet who would inject Greek dactylic hexameter into Latin literature. He later learned Greek privately, in his seventies, while continuing to deliver speeches warning Romans against the corrupting influence of Greek culture. He apparently saw no contradiction in this. Winning the argument was more important than being consistent.\n\nHis censorship of 184 BC is legendary. He expelled senators on moral grounds, taxed luxury goods heavily, cut off private citizens who had illegally tapped the public aqueducts, and in general made himself the most hated and most admired man in Rome simultaneously. The two things frequently go together.\n\nHis most famous habit: in the years leading up to the Third Punic War, he reportedly ended every speech he gave in the Senate -- regardless of the topic -- with the phrase *Ceterum censeo Carthaginem esse delendam*: \"Furthermore, I move that Carthage must be destroyed.\" Whether he was discussing grain prices, road repairs, or the election of magistrates, the speech ended with Carthage. He once brought a fresh fig from Carthage into the Senate chamber to demonstrate how close and fertile the enemy city was, and therefore how dangerous. Carthage was destroyed in 146 BC. Cato died in 149 BC and did not quite live to see it, but he had spent the last two decades of his life making absolutely sure it would happen. Some people plant trees. Cato planted wars.\n\n## Main Works\n\n- **De Agri Cultura** (also called *De Re Rustica*) -- a practical handbook on running a Roman farm: how to plant vineyards, manage slaves, press olive oil, cure hams, and keep oxen healthy. The only complete prose work to survive from before Cicero -- the oldest surviving Latin prose text. [Complete]\n- **Origines** -- Roman history in 7 books, from the origins of Rome through Cato's own time. The first Roman history written in Latin; previous historians had written in Greek. [Fragmentary]\n- **Speeches** -- over 80 are known from ancient sources; substantial fragments survive. Cicero studied them as a young man. [Fragmentary]\n- **Ad Marcum filium** -- precepts addressed to his son on medicine, oratory, law, and farming. [Fragmentary]\n\n## Style and Difficulty\n\nCato is a gift to the Latin student who happens to know what *oleum*, *vinum*, *frumentum*, *vilicus*, and *fundus* mean. If you show up to *De Agri Cultura* with a cheat sheet of agricultural vocabulary -- and this is not a hypothetical, this is a genuine study strategy -- you have cleared the main linguistic obstacle. What remains is archaic morphology, and for that there is no cheat sheet, but there is good news: the archaisms are finite.\n\nThe style of *De Agri Cultura* is utilitarian to the point of self-parody. Cato is not trying to write beautifully. He is trying to be useful. Sentences are short, direct, and structured as commands or factual statements. He writes the same sentence pattern repeatedly without apology, because the point is to convey information, not to demonstrate range. A representative passage runs: *do this. then do this. if you fail to do this, here is what happens. do this.* The repetition is not a stylistic flaw; it is the style.\n\nArchaic forms are the one difficulty that does not yield to preparation. *Quom* for *cum*, *siet* for *sit*, *posiverunt* for *posuerunt*, *foenerari* for *faenerari* -- these surface regularly and are invisible to anyone trained purely on classical Latin. The morphology is roughly a century older than Cicero's, and the gap shows. But unlike Plautus, where the colloquial vocabulary is effectively bottomless, Cato's archaisms follow recognizable patterns. Once catalogued, they stop being surprises.\n\nBy the criteria: lexicon is MEDIUM (agricultural technical vocabulary plus archaic forms, but repetitive and finite); medium is LOW (prose, and the most unadorned prose in the corpus); syntactic complexity is LOW (short paratactic sentences, commands and declarations, almost no subordination); stylistic figures are nearly absent; allusion density is very low; genre burden is LOW (practical manual -- the lowest category on the scale). Tier C is the honest call, and there is nothing embarrassing about it. Cato was not writing for posterity. He was writing for farmers.\n\n## Latin Excerpt, Translation and Analysis\n\n**Latin text** *(The Latin Library, www.thelatinlibrary.com)*\n\n> Est interdum praestare mercaturis rem quaerere, nisi tam periculosum sit, et item foenerari, si tam honestum. Maiores nostri sic habuerunt et ita in legibus posiverunt: furem dupli condemnari, foeneratorem quadrupli. Quanto peiorem civem existimarint foeneratorem quam furem, hinc licet existimare. Et virum bonum quom laudabant, ita laudabant: bonum agricolam bonumque colonum; amplissime laudari existimabatur qui ita laudabatur. Mercatorem autem strenuum studiosumque rei quaerendae existimo, verum, ut supra dixi, periculosum et calamitosum. At ex agricolis et viri fortissimi et milites strenuissimi gignuntur, maximeque pius quaestus stabilissimusque consequitur minimeque invidiosus, minimeque male cogitantes sunt qui in eo studio occupati sunt.\n>\n> *(De Agri Cultura, Praefatio)*\n\n**Italian translation** *(original)*\n\n> A volte conviene guadagnarsi da vivere col commercio, se non fosse tanto rischioso; e col prestito a interesse, se non fosse tanto disonorevole. I nostri antenati la pensavano cosi' e cosi' l'hanno messo per legge: il ladro si condanna al doppio, il prestatore al quadruplo. Quanto considerassero peggiore il prestatore del ladro, lo si puo' capire da questo. E quando lodavano un uomo virtuoso, lo lodavano cosi': buon agricoltore e buon coltivatore; chi veniva lodato in questi termini era considerato il piu' elogiato di tutti. Il mercante lo considero anch'io attivo e dedito al guadagno, ma, come ho gia' detto, e' una vita rischiosa e soggetta a disgrazie. Dagli agricoltori nascono invece gli uomini piu' forti e i soldati piu' tenaci; il guadagno che ne viene e' il piu' onesto, il piu' stabile, il meno invidiato; e chi si dedica a questa occupazione e' il meno portato a pensare male.\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> Sometimes it pays better to seek profit through trade -- if only the life were not so risky -- or through lending at interest, if that were not so dishonorable. Our ancestors thought as much, and wrote it into law: a thief to be fined double, a moneylender fourfold. How much worse they judged a moneylender than a thief, you can judge from this. And when they praised a virtuous man, they praised him thus: good farmer and good tiller of the soil; whoever was praised in those terms was held to be praised in the highest possible way. I consider the merchant vigorous and keen in pursuit of profit -- but, as I said above, the life is risky and prone to ruin. From farmers come the strongest men and the most capable soldiers; the profit earned from the land is the most honorable, the most stable, the least resented; and those devoted to this pursuit are the least inclined to think ill.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThis is the only passage in *De Agri Cultura* where Cato steps back from practical instruction and makes something like a moral argument. It is worth reading carefully, because it reveals how the whole book is meant to be understood: not merely as a technical handbook, but as a statement of Roman values.\n\nThe structure is characteristically Catonian: three short declarative sentences establishing the case against commerce and moneylending; then the law cited as authority. Note *posiverunt* (archaic for *posuerunt*) and *foeneratorem* -- the archaic surface appears immediately, in the very first paragraph. Then the clinching observation is delivered as an invitation rather than a declaration: *hinc licet existimare*, \"from this one may judge.\" Cato does not spell out the conclusion. He lets the argument do its work.\n\n*Quom laudabant, ita laudabant* -- \"when they praised, they praised thus\" -- is the rhetorical high point: a doubled construction that mimics the weight of inherited habit. The superlative chain that closes the passage -- *fortissimi... strenuissimi... stabilissimusque... minimeque invidiosus... minimeque male cogitantes* -- is almost the only extended flourish in the prologue, and it is earned. Cato has been building toward it with short sentences. When the superlatives arrive, they land with cumulative force precisely because everything before them was so compressed.\n\nSyntactically, this is as accessible as archaic Latin gets. The sentences are self-contained, the logic is sequential, and the vocabulary, while including archaic forms (*quom*, *posiverunt*, *foenerari*), makes no technical demands beyond agricultural context. A student with a glossary and an afternoon of preparation can read this passage without difficulty. What they get out of it is, as with all of Cato, something they were not necessarily expecting.\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: C -- Manageable.**\n\nAgricultural vocabulary is the main barrier, and it is a barrier you can build in advance. The archaic forms are the one obstacle a glossary cannot solve, but they are finite, patterned, and learnable. Syntax is paratactic and nearly free of subordination. This is the author you assign to a Latin student who has just survived Caesar and wants to feel competent again -- and who also happens to like olives.\n\n---\n\n# CAECILIUS STATIUS (c. 220-166 BC)\n\n\n![Caecilius Statius](./images/caecilius_statius.jpg)\n*Caecilius Statius *(imaginary reconstruction)**\n## Biography\n\nCaecilius Statius is the essential middle link in a chain almost everyone has heard of -- Plautus to Terence -- and almost no one has read. He is the man who was ranked the greatest Roman comic poet by one ancient critic, called mediocre by another, praised for his moral seriousness by a third, and accused of butchering Menander by a fourth. He survived as roughly 280 lines of fragments and a collection of anecdotes. In some ways this is a more honest legacy than a polished complete corpus.\n\nHe was born around 220 BC in Cisalpine Gaul, among the Insubres -- a Celtic tribe in what is now Lombardy, somewhere in the vicinity of modern Milan. He came to Rome not as a traveler but as a prisoner of war, enslaved during the Roman campaigns against the Insubrian Gauls in the early second century BC. Acquired by a member of the *gens Caecilia*, he took the family name on manumission, keeping *Statius* -- probably his original Gaulish or Oscan personal name -- as his cognomen. So Caecilius Statius was, like Livius Andronicus before him, a freed slave from a conquered people who became one of the foundational figures of Roman literature. Rome had a habit of doing this.\n\nHe was a contemporary and friend of Ennius. When Ennius died in 169 BC, Caecilius died within a year -- perhaps the same year; the sources are slightly confused on the timing. Later Romans spoke of the two men as a literary generation passing together, the torch handed on to Terence.\n\nThat handover is the subject of the most famous story about Caecilius, preserved in Suetonius and retold in Donatus's *Life of Terence*. The young Terence -- poor, shabbily dressed, completely unknown -- came to the elderly Caecilius to read him his first play, the *Andria*. Being poorly dressed, he was told to wait and given a stool near the door while Caecilius finished his dinner. But after the first few lines, Caecilius put down his fork, got up, and invited the young man to sit at the table and eat with him. The story may be apocryphal, but it is the right story: the passing of a comic tradition from one generation to the next, over a dinner the younger man was not initially invited to join.\n\nThen there is the Volcacius Sedigitus problem. In his *Carmen de Poetis* (c. 100 BC), Volcacius ranked the Roman comic poets in order of excellence:\n\n1. Caecilius\n2. Plautus\n3. Naevius\n4. Licinius\n5. Atilius\n6. Terence\n\nTerence. In sixth place. Behind Atilius, who is otherwise a footnote in literary history. Cicero thought this ranking was absurd, Horace quietly ignored it, and modern scholars have spent considerable energy explaining what Volcacius could possibly have meant. The most charitable interpretation is that Volcacius was evaluating stage entertainment for Roman audiences of his own time, in which register Caecilius's broader humor and more elaborate plotting may have been genuinely preferred over Terence's restrained Hellenism. The less charitable interpretation is that Volcacius had terrible taste.\n\nVarro offered a more useful three-way comparison: Caecilius excelled in *argumenta* (plot construction), Plautus in *eloquium* (language and wit), Terence in *ethos* (character and moral texture). This probably captures something real. Quintilian, writing much later, praised Caecilius specifically for *gravitas* -- a moral seriousness unusual in comedy -- and called him the most effective of the Roman comic writers at moving an audience's emotions.\n\nAulus Gellius, in *Noctes Atticae* II.23, sat down with Caecilius's *Plocium* and Menander's *Plocium* side by side and reported feeling depressed by the comparison. After reading Menander's original, Caecilius looked shabby to him: changing things that did not need changing, adding lowbrow jokes where Menander had subtlety, dropping the best moments of the Greek entirely. His verdict is probably unfair -- he was comparing a fragmentarily transmitted adaptation in Latin to a well-preserved original in Greek -- but it stuck, and it is the reason most people who know anything about Caecilius know him primarily as the man who failed to be Menander.\n\nHe did not fail to be Menander. He chose not to be Menander, just as Plautus had. That is a different thing entirely.\n\n## Main Works\n\nAround 42 comedies are attributed to him; none survive complete. Titles known include *Plocium* (The Necklace), *Synephebi* (The Young Men Together), *Epiclerus* (The Heiress), *Hypobolimaeus* (The Changeling), *Triumpus*, *Andria*, *Aethrio*, *Imbrii*. The *Plocium* is the best-attested work, thanks to Gellius. [All fragmentary]\n\n## Style and Difficulty\n\nWhat the fragments suggest: Caecilius wrote closer to Plautus in energy and register than to the restrained Terence, but with more attention to plot architecture and emotional sequence. The *gravitas* Quintilian mentions probably reflects a willingness to let scenes develop feeling rather than rushing to the next joke. The verse is iambic and trochaic, the standard registers of Roman comedy.\n\nBeyond this, reliable assessment is impossible. The NC criteria are met precisely: fragments exist in sufficient quantity to identify a voice, but not to assess difficulty consistently across multiple criteria. What can be said with confidence is that archaic morphology and comic register would have applied, and that the verse -- like all comic verse of the period -- would have required metrical competence to read well. The linguistic distance from Plautus was probably not large.\n\n## Latin Excerpt, Translation and Analysis\n\n**Latin text** *(Caecilius Statius, Plocium, fr. preserved in Aulus Gellius, Noctes Atticae II.23, www.thelatinlibrary.com)*\n\n> is demum miser est, qui aerumnam suam nescit occultare\n> ferre: ita me uxor forma et factis facit, si taceam, tamen indicium.\n> Quae nisi dotem, omnia, quae nolis, habet: qui sapiet, de me discet,\n> qui quasi ad hostes captus liber servio salva urbe atque arce.\n> Quae mihi, quidquid placet, eo privatu vim me servatum.\n> Dum ego eius mortem inhio, egomet vivo mortuus inter vivos.\n> Ea me clam se cum mea ancilla ait consuetum, id me arguit,\n> ita plorando, orando, instando atque obiurgando me obtudit,\n> eam uti venderem; nunc credo inter suas\n> aequalis et cognatas sermonem serit:\n> \"quis vestrarum fuit integra aetatula,\n> quae hoc idem a viro\n> impetrarit suo, quod ego anus modo\n> effeci, paelice ut meum privarem virum?\"\n> haec erunt concilia hodie, differor sermone miser.\n\n**Italian translation** *(original)*\n\n> Davvero infelice e' colui che non sa tenere nascosta la propria miseria:\n> mia moglie, con il suo aspetto e i suoi modi, mi denuncia anche quando taccio.\n> Salvo la dote, ha tutto cio' che non vorresti: chi e' saggio impara da me,\n> io che, uomo libero, servo come un prigioniero di guerra mentre citta' e rocca sono salve.\n> Qualunque cosa mi piaccia, lei me ne priva con la forza, nel mio interesse.\n> Mentre aspetto bramosamente la sua morte, io stesso vivo da morto in mezzo ai vivi.\n> Ha detto che avevo un'intesa segreta con la mia schiava, mi ha accusato,\n> e mi ha tanto logorato con pianti, suppliche, insistenze e rimproveri\n> che l'ho venduta. Ora immagino che stia riferendo alle sue coetanee\n> e parenti: \"quale di voi, nel fiore degli anni,\n> ha mai ottenuto dal proprio marito\n> cio' che io, vecchia com'ero, sono riuscita a ottenere --\n> privare mio marito della sua concubina?\"\n> Questi saranno i discorsi di oggi, e io, povero me, sono fatto a pezzi dai pettegolezzi.\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> Truly wretched is the man who cannot keep his misery hidden:\n> my wife, by her looks and behavior, denounces me even when I say nothing.\n> Apart from her dowry, she has everything you would not want: let the wise man learn from my example --\n> I, a free man, serving like a prisoner of war while the city and citadel stand safe.\n> Whatever pleases me, she deprives me of by force, for my own good.\n> While I eagerly wait for her death, I myself live as a dead man among the living.\n> She accused me of carrying on in secret with my slave girl,\n> and wore me down with weeping, begging, nagging, and scolding until I sold her.\n> Now, I imagine, she is spreading word among her friends\n> and relations of the same age: \"which of you, in the prime of your youth,\n> ever managed to get from her husband\n> what I, old woman that I am, just managed to get --\n> depriving my husband of his mistress?\"\n> That will be the talk today, and I, poor wretch, am being torn apart by gossip.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThis passage is the husband's monologue from Caecilius's *Plocium*, quoted in full by Gellius in *Noctes Atticae* II.23, immediately alongside the corresponding section of Menander's Greek original. Gellius prefers Menander. A reader encountering Caecilius alone, without the comparison, may find themselves less disappointed than Gellius.\n\nThe opening line -- *is demum miser est, qui aerumnam suam nescit occultare* -- circulated in antiquity as a standalone maxim and was quoted independently for its compression and accuracy. \"He alone is truly wretched who cannot conceal his wretchedness\" is both a moral observation and a piece of practical advice for surviving a bad marriage.\n\nThe imagery escalates into comic hyperbole that brushes against something real. *Quasi ad hostes captus liber servio salva urbe atque arce* -- \"I serve like a prisoner of war while the city and citadel stand safe\" -- describes the domestic condition of a Roman man married to a wealthy woman whose dowry gives her leverage he cannot match. Caecilius makes this explicit: *Quae nisi dotem, omnia, quae nolis, habet* -- \"apart from the dowry, she has everything you would not want.\" The *dos* is the trap.\n\n*Dum ego eius mortem inhio, egomet vivo mortuus inter vivos* -- \"while I eagerly await her death, I myself live as a dead man among the living\" -- is probably the best single line in surviving Caecilius. The reversal is clean: the man waiting for his wife to die has, in the waiting, already died first.\n\nNote the fifth line -- *Quae mihi, quidquid placet, eo privatu vim me servatum* -- which is metrically and syntactically troubled. Editors have proposed emendations; the transmitted text is likely corrupt. This is not unusual for fragmentary authors transmitted through quotation, and it is a reminder of the conditions under which we read Caecilius: through a narrow window of other people's books, on a text that has accumulated damage over centuries of copying.\n\nThe metrical form throughout is the trochaic septenarius -- the seven-foot trochaic line Plautus and Caecilius both used for heightened emotional scenes. The rhythm is rapid and insistent, appropriate to the husband's frantic internal inventory of his domestic situation.\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: NC -- Not Comparable.**\n\nAbout 280 lines survive, almost entirely through quotation in other authors. The voice is identifiable and the dramatic approach partially reconstructable, but no complete work exists and the fragments are too sparse to assess difficulty reliably across the full set of criteria. Per-line difficulty would likely place him close to Plautus -- archaic, comic, metrically varied -- but a tier rating built on fifteen-line excerpts in other people's books would be a guess, not an assessment.\n\n---\n\n# PUBLIUS TERENTIUS AFER -- TERENCE (c. 185-159 BC)\n\n\n![Publius Terentius Afer -- Terence](./images/terence.jpg)\n*Publius Terentius Afer -- Terence*\n## Biography\n\nTerence is the only author in this entire document whose complete surviving works you can actually read. Six comedies. All of them. Not \"six out of an estimated forty.\" Not \"six with gaps and lacunae.\" Six complete plays, in order, start to finish, from the first line of the prologue to the closing *plaudite*. In the archaic era, that is a minor miracle, and it matters more than it might seem: it is the only author of the period for whom we can talk about style, development, and technique across a whole body of work rather than guessing from rubble. (Plautus comes close -- twenty-one complete comedies survive, all of them authentic by Varro's reckoning, though scattered fragments from non-Varronian attributions remain incomplete.)\n\nHe was born around 185 BC in Carthage -- hence *Afer*, \"the African\" -- and came to Rome as a slave in the household of the senator Terentius Lucanus. The senator recognized his intelligence, had him educated alongside his own children, and eventually freed him. Terence took his patron's name and kept his cognomen. He was young, he was educated, he was well-connected, and he was probably very good company. Within a few years, he had written six comedies. By around 160 BC, he had apparently left for Greece -- possibly to collect more Menander to translate, possibly just to travel. He never came back. He died sometime around 159 BC, somewhere in Greece or at sea; the ancient sources offer several theories (shipwreck, illness, grief at losing his manuscripts in transit) without agreeing on any of them. He was in his mid-twenties. The six plays were already written. Whatever he was planning to do next, we will never know.\n\nThe accusations against him come in two flavors, both addressed in his prologues. The first was *contaminatio* -- combining multiple Greek originals into a single Latin play. His main critic, the playwright Luscius Lanuvinus, called it literary dishonesty; Terence responded that Naevius, Plautus, and Ennius had done the same and considered them excellent precedents. He did not deny the practice; he denied it was wrong. The second accusation was more personal: that Terence did not write his own plays at all -- that Gaius Laelius and Scipio Aemilianus, the inner circle of the \"Scipionic circle,\" were the real authors. His response in the *Adelphoe* prologue is a masterpiece of non-denial: he considers the association with such admired men no disgrace, and leaves the question open. Modern consensus is that Terence wrote his own plays but genuinely collaborated intellectually with the circle -- which would explain both the accusation and his studied refusal to flatly deny it.\n\n## Main Works\n\nAll six comedies survive complete:\n\n- **Andria** (166 BC) -- The Woman from Andros. His first play; the one he read to Caecilius. A young man in love with an unsuitable girl, a scheming slave, a father who eventually comes around.\n- **Hecyra** (165 and 160 BC) -- The Mother-in-Law. Notoriously flopped twice in a row at its first performances -- the audience left to watch a tightrope walker, and then a gladiatorial show -- and succeeded only on its third staging. A comedy about a marriage that almost didn't happen for reasons nobody will explain.\n- **Heauton Timorumenos** (163 BC) -- The Self-Tormentor. A father who punishes himself for driving his son away. Contains *homo sum*.\n- **Eunuchus** (161 BC) -- The Eunuch. His biggest commercial success; reportedly earned more money on its first day than any previous Latin comedy.\n- **Phormio** (161 BC) -- the scheming parasite Phormio manipulates two fathers into accepting their sons' unsuitable attachments.\n- **Adelphoe** (160 BC) -- The Brothers. Two contrasting fathers, one strict and one permissive, and what their sons turn out to be. Performed at the funeral games of Aemilius Paullus, almost certainly at the direct request of the Scipionic circle.\n\n## Style, Difficulty, and the Concept of *Humanitas*\n\nThe most important thing about Terence, stylistically and philosophically, is a word he did not invent but almost single-handedly defined for Latin literature: *humanitas*.\n\nThe word resists clean translation. It means something like: the quality of being fully and recognizably human -- which in the Roman upper-class context of the 160s BC meant educated, refined, emotionally intelligent, capable of empathy, and freed from the crude reflexes of the merely powerful. It is Cicero's intellectual ideal, Seneca's moral aspiration, and Augustine's theological target. And it is already there, fully formed, in a line of Terence's comedy written in 163 BC.\n\n*homo sum: humani nil a me alienum puto.* \"I am human; I consider nothing human foreign to me.\"\n\nChremes says this to Menedemus to justify why he is sticking his nose into a neighbor's business. It is, on the surface, a joke about being a busybody. But Terence means it structurally. The entire moral architecture of his comedy rests on this principle: that other people's pain is not someone else's problem, that understanding what another person is going through is not sentimental but rational, and that the rigid application of rules to human situations -- by fathers, by law, by social convention -- produces worse outcomes than careful attention to the actual people involved.\n\nThis is what separates Terence from Plautus at the deepest level, and it is worth being precise about the differences.\n\nPlautus writes comedy of situation: the collision of types, each maximally themselves -- the miser as miserly as possible, the braggart soldier as vain as possible, the clever slave as relentlessly clever as possible. The energy is carnivalesque, the vocabulary colloquial and archaically unruly, the cantica (elaborate sung passages in cretic and bacchiac meters) pure spectacle. Everything is built for immediate theatrical impact.\n\nTerence writes comedy of character. A Terentian father is not simply wrong to be strict; he has reasons, and part of the comedy is that everyone, including the strict father, eventually has to acknowledge everyone else's perspective. His Latin is deliberately pure -- Caesar praised his *puri sermonis amor* -- with no deep colloquial register and almost no cantica. His plays are essentially sustained dialogue, written for an educated upper-class audience, which made him commercially less successful than Plautus and considerably more durably influential.\n\nFrom a difficulty standpoint, Terence is more controlled than any other archaic author -- but \"controlled\" is not the same as \"easy.\" The Latin is still from 160 BC. Contracted and archaic forms (*usu'*, *opu'*, *quoiquam* for *cuiquam*, *nil* for *nihil*) appear constantly, and the dialogue is peppered with colloquial interjections -- *eheu!*, *hui!*, *ah!*, *hem!* -- that classical Latin training leaves a reader entirely unprepared for. Syntax is transparent and allusion density is low; the cultural challenge of Roman institutions (patria potestas, the legal status of a freedman, dowry leverage) is real but resolvable with preparation. The archaic sediment, however, is consistent across six complete plays, and it earns him a step above the \"read after Caesar\" comfort zone.\n\n## Latin Excerpt, Translation and Analysis\n\n**Latin text** *(Terence, Heauton Timorumenos, I.i, vv. 75-87, www.thelatinlibrary.com)*\n\n> ME. Chreme, tantumne ab re tuast oti tibi\n> aliena ut cures ea quae nil ad te attinent?\n> CH. homo sum: humani nil a me alienum puto.\n> vel me monere hoc vel percontari puta:\n> rectumst ego ut faciam; non est te ut deterream.\n> ME. mihi sic est usu'; tibi ut opu' factost face.\n> CH. an quoiquamst usus homini se ut cruciet? ME. mihi.\n> CH. si quid laborist nollem. sed quid istuc malist?\n> quaeso, quid de te tantum meruisti? ME. eheu!\n> CH. ne lacruma atque istuc, quidquid est, fac me ut sciam:\n> ne retice, ne verere, crede inquam mihi:\n> aut consolando aut consilio aut re iuvero.\n> ME. scire hoc vis? CH. hac quidem causa qua dixi tibi.\n\n**Italian translation** *(original)*\n\n> ME. Chreme, hai davvero cosi' tanto tempo libero da occuparti delle faccende altrui, che non ti riguardano per niente?\n> CH. Sono un uomo: non considero nulla di umano estraneo a me.\n> Prendila come un avvertimento o come una semplice curiosita':\n> fare questa cosa e' giusto per me; non e' che voglia dissuaderti.\n> ME. Le mie abitudini sono queste; tu fa' come ti conviene.\n> CH. Ma esiste qualcuno a cui faccia bene tormentarsi cosi'? ME. A me.\n> CH. Se c'e' qualche dolore, ne sono dispiaciuto. Ma qual e' questo male?\n> Dimmi, cos'hai fatto di tanto grave a te stesso? ME. Ahime'!\n> CH. Non piangere -- qualunque cosa sia, fammi sapere:\n> non stare in silenzio, non aver paura, credimi:\n> ti aiutero' con parole di conforto, con un consiglio, o con i fatti.\n> ME. Vuoi davvero saperlo? CH. Per il motivo che ti ho detto.\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> ME. Chremes, have you really got so much free time that you concern yourself with other people's affairs, things that have nothing to do with you?\n> CH. I am human: I consider nothing human foreign to me.\n> Take it as advice or as simple curiosity on my part:\n> it is right for me to do this; I am not trying to put you off.\n> ME. My ways are my own; you do as you see fit.\n> CH. Is there anyone for whom tormenting himself is a good idea? ME. For me.\n> CH. I am sorry if there is something painful. But what is this trouble?\n> Tell me, what have you done to deserve this from yourself? ME. Oh.\n> CH. Do not weep -- whatever it is, let me know:\n> do not stay silent, do not be afraid, trust me:\n> I will help you with comfort, with counsel, or with something concrete.\n> ME. Do you really want to know? CH. For the reason I already told you.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThe scene opens with Menedemus -- a neighbor Chremes barely knows -- performing hard manual labor in his fields despite being a wealthy old man of sixty. Chremes cannot leave it alone. His justification, when Menedemus asks why he is meddling: *homo sum: humani nil a me alienum puto.*\n\nThe line is iambic senarius -- the workhorse meter of Terentian dialogue, which mimics the natural stress patterns of spoken Latin well enough that Cicero cited Terence as evidence that good prose and good iambic verse share the same rhythmic instincts. The syntax is clean and direct. There is no difficult vocabulary, no hyperbaton, no ellipsis. Even the compressed *nil* for *nihil* is standard colloquial Latin, not an archaic form. This is, by the standards of the archaic period, exceptionally readable Latin.\n\nThe content, however, is doing philosophical work. *Humani nil a me alienum puto* is not just an expression of nosiness. It is a claim about the scope of moral attention: everything a human being experiences is, by virtue of that shared humanity, also something I am capable of understanding and therefore something I have some obligation to engage with. The Stoic underpinning -- that all humans share a common rational nature that makes them mutually intelligible and mutually responsible -- was already circulating in the Roman Hellenism of the Scipionic circle. Terence puts it in the mouth of a comedy character justifying why he is pestering a man he met last week.\n\nNote that Menedemus's answer is not a refutation. He simply says *mihi sic est usu'* -- \"those are my ways.\" He is not arguing that Chremes is wrong to care; he is asserting the right to privacy. The comedy that follows -- his story of driving his son away and punishing himself for it by working like a slave -- proves Chremes right to ask. The *humanitas* principle wins.\n\nThe scene also models Terentian dialogue: two voices, each grammatically complete, trading control of the conversation in short exchanges. This is not the voluble verbal flooding of Plautus. It is measured, almost Socratic. The effect is that the characters feel like people thinking in real time rather than performers delivering prepared material.\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: B -- Difficult.**\n\nTerence is the most controlled Latin writer of the archaic era, but control is not the same as accessibility. The archaic morphology is consistent -- contracted forms, pre-classical spellings, colloquial interjections scattered throughout the dialogue -- and it is the kind of difficulty that compounds over a long read: one *eheu!* or *usu'* is a footnote; six complete plays of them is a genuine lexical and phonological adjustment. Syntax is transparent and allusion density is low, but the cultural context (patria potestas, dowry law, the social ladder from slave to freedman) needs to be in place before the comedies make full sense. Placed above Naevius for the sustained archaic register across a complete and extensive corpus; well below Plautus, whose colloquial depth and cantica represent a separate category of difficulty entirely.\n\n---\n\n# MARCUS PACUVIUS AND LUCIUS ACCIUS\n\n### Marcus Pacuvius (c. 220-130 BC) and Lucius Accius (170-86 BC)\n\nRoman tragedy had two giants, and we have almost nothing by either of them. This is one of antiquity's more irritating losses: the Greeks gave us Aeschylus, Sophocles, and Euripides in large and mostly readable quantities; Rome gave us Pacuvius and Accius and then, over several centuries of copying and neglect, quietly lost the plays. What survives are fragments, critical assessments, and one anecdote that neatly captures the relationship between the two men and, through them, the whole tradition they represented.\n\n## Biographies\n\n\n![Marcus Pacuvius](./images/pacuvius.jpg)\n*Marcus Pacuvius *(imaginary reconstruction)**\n**Marcus Pacuvius** was born around 220 BC in Brundisium -- modern Brindisi, at the heel of Italy. He was the nephew of Ennius: their connection was through Ennius's sister, making Pacuvius the closest thing we have to a family lineage in early Latin literature. He came to Rome sometime in the second century BC, where he worked first as a painter -- Pliny the Elder mentions a picture he made in the Temple of Hercules in the Forum Boarium, the cattle market, which was still visible in Pliny's time, four centuries later -- before establishing himself as the greatest tragic playwright of his generation. He was admitted to the circles of the Roman aristocratic elite: a friend of Gaius Laelius, the same Laelius connected to the Scipionic circle and to Terence.\n\nHe wrote thirteen tragedies known by name, all adapted from Greek models (*fabulae cothurnatae* -- \"high-boot plays,\" after the footwear of Greek tragic actors), plus at least one *fabula praetexta*, the Paulus, a Roman historical tragedy about Aemilius Paullus and the Battle of Pydna. He lived to approximately ninety-one years old, which, for the ancient world, is practically supernatural. In his extreme old age he retired to Tarentum in southern Italy, where he spent his last years. He was still writing, or at least receiving visitors, when a much younger Accius came through.\n\n\n![Lucius Accius](./images/accius.jpg)\n*Lucius Accius *(imaginary reconstruction)**\n**Lucius Accius** was born in 170 BC in Pisaurum -- modern Pesaro, on the Adriatic coast. The date is unusually precise for an ancient author: Accius himself apparently mentioned being born during the ludi Romani of 170 BC. His father was a freedman -- a former slave -- which made Accius the son of someone who had himself been property. He used this detail, or at least did not hide from it: he was one of the most combative and self-assured literary figures of the Roman republic.\n\nHe was, by any measure, extraordinarily prolific. More than forty-five tragedies are attributed to him, plus historical plays, grammatical treatises, and a major work on Roman theatrical history and chronology called the *Didascalica*. He was a member of the *collegium poetarum*, the official guild of Roman poets and playwrights. The collegium had a hall where members met, and there is a famous story about what happened when Julius Caesar's father -- the praetor, not the dictator -- entered that hall. Every other member stood. Accius did not. When asked why, he explained that he had not stood when he entered the room, and therefore saw no reason to stand for others entering it. The social calculus here is extraordinary: a man whose father had been a slave, refusing to rise for a Roman magistrate of consular family, in a room full of poets, on a point of artistic dignity. Nobody, apparently, threw him out.\n\nHe outlived almost everyone. He was still alive, elderly and nearly deaf, when Cicero was a young man in the 80s BC -- Cicero says he knew Accius personally and attended readings of his plays. This means Accius's career stretched across a century of Roman history, from Ennius's death to Sulla's dictatorship.\n\nThe anecdote that best captures both men at once is preserved by Aulus Gellius. When Accius was on his way to Asia, he stopped to visit the very elderly Pacuvius in Tarentum. Pacuvius asked him to read from his new tragedy, the *Atreus*. Accius read it. Pacuvius praised the language as powerful and elevated but found it perhaps too harsh, too rough -- *truculentiora*, too savage. Accius responded that he intended to keep writing that way and expected to improve with age. \"You are right,\" said Pacuvius; \"for I notice that in my case the fruit has ripened, but in yours it is still forming.\" (*mea, inquit, matura, tua, ut video, viridisunt*.) It is, in miniature, the whole story of literary generations: the older writer who has arrived at mastery warning the younger one that ambition alone is not mellowness, while the younger one cheerfully ignores the advice.\n\n## Main Works\n\n**Pacuvius:** Thirteen tragedies known by name including *Niptra* (Ulysses Wounded), *Teucer*, *Antiopa*, *Hermiona*, *Dulorestes*, *Chryses*, *Iliona*; one *fabula praetexta* (*Paulus*). [All fragmentary]\n\n**Accius:** Over 45 tragedies including *Atreus*, *Medea*, *Antigona*, *Clytaemestra*, *Philocteta*, *Brutus* (*praetexta*), *Decius* (*praetexta*); also *Didascalica* (literary history) and grammatical works. [All dramatic works fragmentary; *Didascalica* survives only in citations]\n\n## Style and Difficulty\n\nQuintilian's paired assessment in *Institutio Oratoria* X.1 is the most useful starting point: Pacuvius, he says, is the most learned (*doctissimus*) of the Roman tragic poets, with genuine weight and solemnity in his verse -- but if he were only clearer and more concise, he would be comparable to Sophocles. Accius he rates as more powerful in sheer force, with a grandeur and vigor that some readers prefer to any other tragic poet -- but rough and uneven in execution. The shorthand that stuck in antiquity was: Pacuvius for *gravitas* and *doctrina*, Accius for *vis* and *ardor*.\n\nBoth wrote what Romans called *fabulae cothurnatae* -- adaptations of Greek tragic plots, often from Euripides or Sophocles, given Roman Latin costumes. The literary challenge was translating not just the stories but the tone: Greek tragedy's elevated diction, its choral passages, its metaphysical weight, into a language and culture that had no indigenous tragic tradition before the 240s BC. Both managed it at a high level, if Quintilian is to be believed.\n\nNeither is assessable for difficulty in any reliable way. The fragments are sufficient to confirm that both wrote elevated, archaic dramatic verse -- dense with allusion, grammatically demanding, metrically complex (the iambic senarius of comedy gives way to longer and more elaborate meters in tragedy) -- but the surviving material is too sparse and too decontextualized (single lines torn out of plays we cannot read) to rate them consistently across multiple criteria.\n\n## Latin Excerpts, Translations and Analyses\n\n### Pacuvius -- *Niptra* (Ulysses Wounded)\n\n**Latin text** *(Pacuvius, Niptra, fr. preserved in Cicero, Tusculanae Disputationes II.50, www.thelatinlibrary.com)*\n\n> Conqueri fortunam adversam, non lamentari decet.\n> Id viri est officium, fletus muliebri ingenio additus.\n\n**Italian translation** *(original)*\n\n> Lamentarsi della sorte avversa e' lecito; piangere su di essa, no.\n> Questo e' il compito di un uomo; le lacrime appartengono all'animo femminile.\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> To complain of adverse fortune is fitting; to lament it is not.\n> That is a man's duty; weeping belongs to a woman's nature.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThese two lines close the *Niptra* -- \"The Washing\" or \"The Foot-Washing,\" the name referring to the scene in which Ulysses, wounded by a spear thrown by his own son Telegonus (who did not know his father's identity), is being carried by attendants. The play adapted a story from the lost Greek epic tradition. Cicero quotes the passage in *Tusculanae Disputationes* II to illustrate the proper philosophical response to pain: not stoic silence, not theatrical weeping, but the controlled articulation of complaint without collapse into grief.\n\nThe lines are in iambic senarii. The syntax is clean -- a parallel construction (*conqueri... non lamentari*) resolved by two defining statements. The language is unambiguous, almost aphoristic. This is precisely why Cicero found them quotable: they state a Roman moral position in Roman dramatic verse with complete economy.\n\nThe gendered framing -- weeping as feminine, controlled complaint as masculine -- is entirely conventional for the period and for Roman tragedy generally. What is Pacuvian is the precision: *conqueri* (to complain, to voice grievance) is explicitly permitted; *lamentari* (to lament, to give way to grief) is not. The distinction is fine enough to be philosophically interesting rather than merely macho.\n\n---\n\n### Accius -- *Atreus*\n\n**Latin text** *(Accius, Atreus, fr. preserved in Cicero, De Officiis I.97, www.thelatinlibrary.com)*\n\n> oderint, dum metuant.\n\n**Italian translation** *(original)*\n\n> Mi odino pure, purche' mi temano.\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> Let them hate, so long as they fear.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThree words. One of the most consequential three-word phrases in Latin literature.\n\nCicero quotes it in *De Officiis* I.97 while discussing theatrical decorum: it would be outrageous, he says, to hear a just ruler like Aeacus or Minos say this. But spoken by Atreus -- the murderer of his brother's children, who cooked them and served them at a feast -- it is exactly right. The line fits the character, which is what Accius intended. Tragedy is not obliged to put good thoughts in the mouths of evil people.\n\nThe line became famous long before Caligula. It circulated as the definition of tyrannical government throughout the late Republic. Cicero treats it as self-evidently wrong: a ruler who governs through fear rather than love has already failed as a political figure and as a human being. The Stoic argument is that *metuant* produces compliance, not loyalty, and that compliance based on fear collapses the moment the fear source is removed. *Oderint, dum metuant* is not a governing philosophy; it is a confession that you have no legitimacy.\n\nThen, approximately two centuries after Accius wrote it, Caligula made it his favorite saying. Suetonius records this in *Caligula* 30. The joke that history kept playing -- that the most explicit theatrical statement of what a tyrant sounds like would be adopted enthusiastically by an actual Roman emperor -- would probably not have surprised Accius. He had, after all, refused to stand up for a magistrate. He understood exactly what kind of men ended up in positions of power.\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: NC -- Not Comparable (both).**\n\nThe fragments of Pacuvius and Accius together amount to a few hundred lines, almost entirely isolated from their dramatic contexts. The per-line difficulty is assessable -- archaic elevated tragic verse, metrically varied, allusion-dense, with Pacuvian obscurity compounding the challenge -- but there is no basis for a reliable cross-criteria rating without extended connected texts. Both are NC. The anecdotes are better preserved than the plays.\n\n---\n\n# GAIUS LUCILIUS (c. 180-102 BC)\n\n\n![Gaius Lucilius](./images/lucilius.jpg)\n*Gaius Lucilius *(imaginary reconstruction)**\n## Biography\n\nGaius Lucilius was born around 180 BC in Suessa Aurunca, a small town in Campania, and died around 102 BC in Naples. He was of equestrian rank -- wealthy, well-connected, and emphatically not obliged to worry about what anyone thought of him. But even among equestrians, Lucilius had a particular kind of insurance: he was the personal friend, and for a time the companion-in-arms, of Publius Cornelius Scipio Aemilianus.\n\nScipio Aemilianus is the man who destroyed Carthage in 146 BC. He is also the man who destroyed Numantia in 133 BC. In the 130s BC he was the single most powerful figure in Rome, commanding a circle that included the historian Polybius, the philosopher Panaetius, the statesman Laelius, and in a more junior capacity a young writer from Campania with opinions about everyone. Lucilius served with Scipio at Numantia; Velleius Paterculus mentions him in this context, and the experience apparently confirmed his view that with the right connections you could say essentially whatever you wanted about whoever you liked.\n\nThis view turned out to be correct. Lucilius wrote thirty books of *Saturae*, in which he attacked sitting senators, consuls, generals, and public figures by name, described their failings in specific and often unflattering terms, and generally treated Rome's political class as raw material for entertainment. Nobody took him to court. Nobody had him exiled like Naevius. The relationship with Scipio was presumably part of the reason; the equestrian rank provided further insulation; and it is also possible that the satires were simply so entertaining that their targets preferred not to draw additional attention to themselves by complaining. In any case, the genre of Roman personal satire -- the one Quintilian would later call *satura tota nostra est*, entirely ours and not borrowed from the Greeks -- was built on Lucilius's combination of genuine literary talent and exceptionally convenient social position.\n\nAfter Scipio's death in 129 BC (sudden, unexplained, and widely suspected to be political murder), Lucilius continued writing for another twenty-five years. He died in Naples around 102 BC at a considerable age, and Jerome records that he received a public funeral. The city had apparently made peace with the idea of a man who spent his entire career being rude about everyone in verse.\n\n## Most Important Works\n\n**Saturae** (30 books; fragmentary, approximately 1,300 lines surviving across hundreds of isolated fragments)\n\nThe thirty books of *Saturae* are the founding document of Roman satirical literature. Subject matter ranges widely: philosophy (Lucilius had Stoic sympathies, probably absorbed from the Scipionic circle), literary criticism, gastronomy, travel, erotic content, and above all direct personal attacks on named contemporaries. Lucilius named names, described specific actions, and drew character portraits of real people in ways that would have been legally and physically risky for anyone without his connections.\n\nThe metrical organization of the books is counterintuitive: books 26-30 (composed first, despite the numbering) use dactylic hexameter; the later books use trochaic septenarii, iambic senarii, and other meters. Horace would eventually establish hexameter as the default meter for Latin satire, following Lucilius's early precedent and setting aside his later experimentalism.\n\n## Writing Style and Difficulty\n\nLucilius is the inventor of a genre, which means reading him involves meeting Latin at an early, rough, pre-canonical moment in that genre's history.\n\nThe lexical difficulty is real. His Latin is archaic and colloquial simultaneously -- a specific combination quite different from Cato's archaic prose (formal, compressed) or Plautus's colloquial Latin (theatrical, exuberant). Lucilius sounds like an educated Roman talking to equals at dinner in the second century BC, while inserting Greek words whenever the Latin equivalent seems insufficient or less precise. He did this constantly. Horace found it irritating, criticizing the habit explicitly in *Satires* I.10: mixing Greek and Latin, he says, is no great achievement, and a serious Roman author should commit to one language. Lucilius, composing about a century earlier, evidently found the code-switching perfectly natural. For the modern reader, the result is that working through his fragments requires at minimum a basic familiarity with Greek vocabulary on top of archaic Latin morphology and pre-classical spelling conventions.\n\nThe deeper difficulty, however, is topicality. Lucilius's satires attacked real people, by name, for specific and contemporary reasons. The humor depended on the audience knowing who these people were and what exactly they had done. We do not. Almost none of the named targets can be securely identified, and even where identification is possible, the specific offense motivating the attack is usually lost. A line that was devastatingly accurate about a particular senator in 130 BC now requires three paragraphs of scholarly apparatus to explain why it might once have been funny.\n\nThen there is Horace's verdict, which has shaped every subsequent reading of Lucilius and is worth engaging with directly. In *Satires* I.4, Horace traces Lucilius's descent from Greek Old Comedy (Eupolis, Cratinus, Aristophanes) and then delivers his characteristically double assessment: Lucilius was *emunctae naris* -- keen-nosed, quick to smell out a target -- but also *durus conponere versus*, rough and hard to write well. And: he dictated two hundred verses per hour *stans pede in uno*, standing on one foot. The *stans pede in uno* was probably proverbial for doing something effortlessly, without pausing to think. Two hundred verses an hour, on one foot, *ut magnum* -- as if that were some great achievement. The result: *cum flueret lutulentus, erat quod tollere velles* -- when he flowed muddy, there was always something you would want to cut.\n\nHorace modeled his entire satirical career on Lucilius. He also could not stop pointing out everything he would have done differently. This is what literary admiration looks like when practiced by someone with very firm opinions about craft.\n\n## Latin Excerpt and Analysis\n\n**Latin text** *(Lucilius, Saturae, frr. 1342-1354 Krenkel, preserved in Lactantius, Divinae Institutiones VI.5)*\n\n> Virtus, Albine, est pretium persolvere verum\n> quis in versamur, quis vivimus rebus, potesse,\n> virtus est homini scire id quod quaeque habeat res,\n> virtus scire homini rectum, utile quid sit, honestum,\n> quae bona, quae mala item quid inutile, turpe, inhonestum,\n> virtus quaerendae finem re scire modumque,\n> virtus divitiis pretium persolvere posse,\n> virtus id dare quod re ipsa debetur honori,\n> hostem esse atque inimicum hominum morumque malorum\n> contra defensorem hominum morumque bonorum,\n> hos magni facere, his bene velle, his vivere amicum,\n> commoda praeterea patriai prima putare,\n> deinde parentum, tertia iam postremaque nostra.\n\n**Italian translation** *(original)*\n\n> Virtus, Albino, e' poter pagare il giusto prezzo nelle cose in cui ci muoviamo, nelle cose in cui viviamo; virtus e' per l'uomo conoscere cio' che ogni cosa ha in se stessa; virtus e' conoscere per l'uomo cio' che e' retto, utile, onesto; quali cose siano buone, quali parimenti malvagie, inutili, turpi, disoneste; virtus e' conoscere il fine e la misura del cercare; virtus e' poter dare il giusto valore alle ricchezze; virtus e' dare cio' che in realta' e' dovuto all'onore; essere nemico e avversario degli uomini e dei costumi malvagi, e al contrario difensore degli uomini e dei costumi buoni; tenere costoro in grande stima, voler loro bene, vivere come loro amico; e poi ritenere i vantaggi della patria al primo posto, poi quelli dei genitori, e al terzo posto, infine, i nostri.\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> Virtue, Albinus, is being able to pay the fair price in the things we deal in, the things we live by; virtue is knowing for a man what each thing contains within itself; virtue is knowing what is right, useful, and honourable; what things are good and what are equally bad, useless, base, dishonourable; virtue is knowing the limit and measure of what is sought; virtue is being able to give wealth its proper value; virtue is giving what is in truth owed to honour; being enemy and adversary to bad men and bad customs, and on the contrary defender of good men and good customs; holding these in high regard, wishing them well, living as their friend; and moreover putting the advantages of the fatherland first, then those of our parents, and our own interests third and last.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThis is the virtus fragment -- the most philosophically substantial passage to survive from Lucilius's thirty books, and one of the most discussed texts in all of archaic Latin. It is preserved by Lactantius in his *Divinae Institutiones* (VI.5), who quotes it as a model pagan definition of virtue before arguing that it falls short of the Christian one. That Lactantius kept it is the reason we have it at all. Neither The Latin Library nor Splash Latino carries a dedicated Lucilius page; the text here follows the standard scholarly edition of Krenkel (1970), which reconstructs the fragments from their ancient sources.\n\nThe fragment opens with a direct address to \"Albine\" -- almost certainly a friend or associate of Lucilius, possibly a member of the Scipionic circle. The conversational frame is characteristic: satire as dinner-table philosophy between equals, not public oration. We are in the middle of an argument, not at a lectern.\n\nThe dominant rhetorical figure is anaphora: *virtus* repeated six times, each instance opening a new clause in what becomes a catalogue definition. The structure is broadly tripartite: *virtus* as practical knowledge (lines 1-5), *virtus* as practical conduct (lines 6-8), and *virtus* as social and civic duty (lines 9-13).\n\nThe syntax of the opening lines is an immediate index of the difficulty. Line 2 -- *quis in versamur, quis vivimus rebus, potesse* -- is one of the most compressed in the fragment: *quis* here is the locative/dative plural of the relative pronoun (equivalent to *in quibus*), and *potesse* is the archaic infinitive of *posse*. The clause means \"being able [to pay the fair price] in the things in which we move, in the things by which we live\" -- but reaching that reading requires recognizing the archaic infinitive form, resolving the ellipsis carried over from line 1, and understanding *versamur* in its idiomatic sense of \"to be engaged in, to move in a sphere.\" None of these is individually fatal; all at once, in two lines, without surrounding context, this is representative of what the rest of the fragments will do to you.\n\n*Patriai* in line 12 is the archaic genitive of *patria* (-ai for -ae), inherited from the oldest Latin declension patterns and shared with Lucretius. It is a deliberate register marker: old-fashioned, solemn, Roman in the deepest sense of the word.\n\nThe closing three lines deliver the climax. After the long catalogue of intellectual and social virtues, Lucilius ends with a priority ranking: the fatherland first (*patriai prima*), then parents (*parentum*), our own interests last (*postremaque nostra*). The compression of the final line -- *tertia iam postremaque nostra* -- lands with unusual force. *Iam* is idiomatic here (\"and in fact,\" \"yes, and\"), and *postremaque* (last of all) doubles the weight of *tertia* (third). Lucilius is not inventing a new ethics; he is articulating, with remarkable precision, the inherited Roman hierarchy of obligations -- and making absolutely clear where the individual stands in relation to it.\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: A -- Very Difficult.**\n\nThree criteria push Lucilius into A territory simultaneously. The lexicon is genuinely demanding: archaic pre-classical morphology, colloquial contractions, and Greek insertions throughout without advance notice. The allusion density is high in a way specific to personal satire: the targets are topical and contemporary, meaning the humor depends on knowledge that is now almost entirely lost and cannot be recovered by preparation alone. The fragmentary state of the corpus eliminates the contextual support that normally makes sustained reading easier -- virtually every passage must be interpreted without surrounding text. The surviving material is substantial enough in total (around 1,300 lines) that NC is not warranted; the difficulty is assessable and consistently high. But the absence of extended connected text means there is nowhere to find firm footing, and the difficulty never lets up.\n\n---\n\n# POMPONIUS BONONIENSIS AND QUINTUS NOVIUS\n\n### Lucius Pomponius Bononiensis (fl. c. 90 BC) and Quintus Novius (fl. c. 90-80 BC)\n\n*Lesser-Known Authors*\n\n---\n\nThe Atellan farce had two fathers, and we have almost nothing by either of them. This is somewhat less devastating than the loss of Pacuvius and Accius, because the *fabulae Atellanae* were never aiming for literary immortality in the first place -- they were the low comedy of festivals and market days, performed by actors in exaggerated masks for audiences who wanted to laugh at the same disasters happening to the same characters they had always laughed at. But the loss still stings, because from the fragments it is clear that both men were genuinely funny in ways that do not require scholarly apparatus to appreciate. The jokes land. That is rarer than it sounds across two thousand years.\n\nPomponius and Novius are always mentioned together by ancient critics, because they did the same thing at the same time: they took the Atellan farce from an improvised performance tradition in Oscan into a scripted Latin literary genre. Before them, the *fabulae Atellanae* were unwritten and performed in the native language of Campania; after them, they had fixed Latin texts. This is not a minor contribution. It is the difference between folk music and a published score.\n\n## Biographies\n\n\n![Lucius Pomponius Bononiensis](./images/pomponius.jpg)\n*Lucius Pomponius Bononiensis *(imaginary reconstruction)**\n**Lucius Pomponius Bononiensis** -- the cognomen tells you everything that is known about his origins: he was from Bononia, the city on the Via Aemilia that is now Bologna. This makes him, to the best of our knowledge, the only significant Latin literary figure from what was then technically Cisalpine Gaul, a region that would not receive full Roman citizenship until 49 BC under Caesar. Whether this made him an outsider in Rome's literary circles, or whether nobody particularly cared, we do not know. What we do know is that he was active around the time of Sulla, worked alongside Quintus Novius, and proceeded to do something no one had done before: he wrote down the Atellan farce.\n\nThe *fabulae Atellanae* were one of the oldest forms of popular Italian entertainment. They took their name from Atella, a town in Campania between Capua and Naples, originally performed in Oscan by improvising actors wearing fixed masks who played stock characters in loose comic scenarios. Rome had been importing and watching *Atellanae* for generations before Pomponius's time. They were the ancient equivalent of television: everyone had seen them, nobody would have claimed they were high art, and the audience knew exactly who was going to do what to whom because the characters never changed.\n\nThe four stock characters were: **Maccus**, the fool, the glutton, the perpetually unsuccessful schemer -- a man who enters every situation with a plan and leaves it without his pants; **Bucco**, the loud-mouthed braggart with puffed-out cheeks (*buccae*), all talk and no thought; **Pappus**, the elderly man, inevitably rich, inevitably cuckolded, and inevitably convinced that his young wife is faithful; and **Dossennus**, the hunchback trickster, a figure of sly intelligence who is funnier than he is clever and cleverer than he looks.\n\nWhat Pomponius did -- and this is his specific literary contribution -- was write it down. He gave the *Atellanae* a fixed script, a predetermined plot, and formal metrical structure. He transformed an improvised folk entertainment into a scripted literary genre. Approximately seventy play titles are attributed to him; around two hundred verses survive.\n\nAncient critics recognized the achievement. Fronto, writing to Marcus Aurelius, singles out Novius and Pomponius for their mastery of *verba rusticana et jocularia ac ridicularia* -- rustic, comic, and absurd vocabulary. Seneca the Elder (*Controversiae* 7.3.9) credits Pomponius specifically with inventing wordplay based on the double meaning of a single word. Velleius Paterculus places him among notable writers of the Sullan period. Macrobius quotes the *Kalendae Martiae* in the *Saturnalia*. As for what Pomponius actually looked like, thought, or cared about: unknown. He is, appropriately for a genre built on stock characters, something of a stock character himself in the history of literature -- present only in other people's footnotes and his own fragments.\n\n\n![Quintus Novius](./images/novius.jpg)\n*Quintus Novius *(imaginary reconstruction)**\n**Quintus Novius** is an even greater ghost than Pomponius. No ancient source tells us where he was born, who his family was, or how he died. We know he was active at roughly the same time as Pomponius -- the late Republic, around 90-80 BC -- and that the two men are consistently named together as the founders of literary Atellan comedy. Beyond that, biography is silence.\n\nWhat the fragments reveal is a writer who was, if anything, even sharper with verbal wit than Pomponius. Cicero quotes Novius directly in *De Oratore* (2.279) -- a scene from an unnamed play in which a father is weeping and someone asks why:\n\n> quid ploras, pater? -- mirum ni canem / condemnatus sum.\n> \"Why are you crying, father?\" -- \"No wonder -- I have been convicted. A thousand sestertii.\"\n\nThe joke is constructed with a professional's timing: the question is asked, the emotional register is established, the explanation is delayed, and when it arrives it is simultaneously trivial and entirely sufficient. Cicero uses it as a textbook example of how comedy produces laughter through surprise and reversal -- the same mechanisms he is analyzing in a treatise on oratory. That Cicero, with his elaborate theory of wit, chose a Novius fragment to illustrate his point is no minor endorsement.\n\nFronto also quotes Novius separately from Pomponius (*Epistulae ad M. Caes.* 4.6.1H), praising a fragment about the throat (*fauces*) as a prime example of rustic vocabulary. Macrobius quotes Novius in the same *Saturnalia* discussion where he quotes Pomponius's *Kalendae Martiae*, suggesting both wrote comedy associated with seasonal celebrations. Approximately forty-three play titles are attributed to Novius; roughly one hundred lines survive.\n\n## Most Important Works\n\n**Pomponius:** Around seventy plays, none surviving complete. The major groupings are:\n\nThe **Maccus** series: *Maccus*, *Macci Gemini* (The Twin Maccuses -- a premise that sounds like a recipe for structural chaos), *Maccus Miles* (Maccus as Soldier), *Maccus Sequester* (Maccus as Intermediary), and *Maccus Virgo* (Maccus as Virgin -- which went exactly as well as the title suggests).\n\nThe **Pappus** series: *Pappus Agricola* (Pappus the Farmer), *Pappus Praeteritus* (Pappus Passed Over), *Sponsa Pappi* (Pappus's Bride), and *Hirnea Pappi* (Pappus's Wine Jug).\n\nThe **Bucco** series: *Bucco Adoptatus* (Bucco the Adopted Son) and *Bucco Auctoratus* (Bucco Under Contract).\n\nStandalone titles: *Galli Transalpini* (The Transalpine Gauls), *Fullones* (The Cloth-Fullers), *Philosophia*, *Dotata* (The Woman with a Dowry), *Leno* (The Pimp), and *Pannuceati* (The Ragged Men). [All fragmentary]\n\n**Novius:** Around forty-three plays, none surviving complete. The stock-character series includes *Maccus*, *Maccus Copo* (Maccus the Innkeeper), *Maccus Exul* (Maccus the Exile), *Pappus Praeteritus* (Pappus Passed Over -- the same title as one of Pomponius's, which tells you something about genre conventions), and *Duo Dossenni* (The Two Dossenni).\n\nStandalone titles: *Agricola* (The Farmer), *Asinus* (The Donkey), *Exodium* (The Finale -- possibly a generic term for the Atellan piece performed at the end of a theatrical program), *Fullones* (also the same title as Pomponius's -- the fulling workshop was evidently irresistible for both of them), *Funus* (The Funeral), *Gallinaria* (The Chicken Coop), *Gemini* (The Twins), *Milites Pometinenses* (The Soldiers from Pometia), *Parcus* (The Miser), *Surdus* (The Deaf Man), *Tabellaria* (The Tablet Carrier), *Vindemiatores* (The Grape-Harvesters), and *Virgo Praegnans* (The Pregnant Virgin). [All fragmentary]\n\n## Style and Difficulty\n\nThe ancient critics who mention Pomponius and Novius tend to mention them in the same breath, and for good reason: they were working in the same genre for the same audience with the same stock of characters. Fronto's *verba rusticana et jocularia ac ridicularia* applies equally to both. Both wrote predominantly in iambic senarii and trochaic septenarii. Both preferred short lines, imperative-heavy syntax, rapid dialogue, and jokes that land on the last word.\n\nWhere a distinction can be attempted, it is this: Pomponius seems to have leaned toward situational and physical comedy -- the humor of Maccus in a disguise, of Pappus comprehensively deceived, of workers in a fulling shop giving each other contradictory instructions. Novius had a sharper tongue. The condemned-man fragment Cicero quotes is purely verbal; the joke is entirely in the structure of the disclosure. Seneca's attribution of wordplay invention to Pomponius is Pomponius-specific; Cicero's endorsement of Novius as a model for well-constructed comic reversal is Novius-specific. The difference, if real, is roughly the difference between a pratfall and a punchline. In the Atellan farce, both were necessary.\n\nThe stock character structure means that very little context is needed to grasp what is happening in any given scene. When Pappus Agricola learns that someone is driving his wife \"like a donkey at the millstone,\" the situation is immediately legible. Atellan comedy is not subtle. It is not trying to be.\n\nMetrically, the fragments are predominantly iambic senarii and trochaic septenarii -- the same meters used by Plautus and Terence, making the rhythmic structure familiar to anyone who has read those authors. Syntactically, both writers operate with great simplicity: imperatives, short questions, direct insults. There is no room for periodic sentences or hyperbaton when performance demands immediate comprehension.\n\nThe main difficulty for a modern student is vocabulary. The rustic and colloquial words in Pomponius and Novius do not appear in standard Latin courses, and many are hapax legomena -- single attestations preserved only because a grammarian needed an example. Nonius Marcellus is the most important ancient source for both, preserving fragments in his lexicographical *De Compendiosa Doctrina* not as literary monuments but as illustrations of unusual words. Context is almost entirely absent: we know the lines but rarely who is speaking, to whom, or why.\n\n## Latin Excerpts, Translations and Analyses\n\n### Pomponius Bononiensis -- *Fullones* (The Cloth-Fullers)\n\n**Latin text** *(Pomponius Bononiensis, Fullones, fr. 48-50 Ribbeck, preserved by Nonius Marcellus 472M)*\n\n*Source note: Neither The Latin Library nor Splash Latino has a dedicated Pomponius Bononiensis page. The text is drawn from PHI Latin Texts (phi0618.phi001), which uses the Ribbeck edition as its base text. The ancient source is Nonius Marcellus, the 4th-century AD grammarian, who preserves this passage under his entry for the word* conplectite *in* De Compendiosa Doctrina.\n\n> Quin ergo, quando conuenit, conplectite?\n> Mi frater, salueto! -- O soror, salue, mea!\n> Facite ut ignis feruat, ligna insipite, far concidite.\n\n**Italian translation** *(original)*\n\n> Su allora, visto che e' il momento, abbracciatevi!\n> Fratello mio, salve! -- Oh sorella mia, salve!\n> Fate in modo che il fuoco bolla, mettete dentro i legni, tritate il farro.\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> Come on then, since it is the right moment, go ahead and embrace!\n> My brother, hello! -- Oh my sister, hello!\n> Make sure the fire boils, feed in the wood, chop up the grain.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThe play is the *Fullones* -- the Cloth-Fullers. A *fullo* was a laundry worker who cleaned and finished woolen cloth by treading it in large vats filled with water, clay, and stale urine (the ammonia content being essential to the process). Roman fulling workshops were a fixture of city life; the fullers of Pompeii were famous enough to paint political campaign slogans on walls. Choosing them as the subject of an Atellan farce is characteristic: this is comedy from the world of physical, smelly, manual labor, the exact milieu where Maccus and Bucco would feel most at home.\n\nThe fragment was preserved because Nonius Marcellus needed to cite the imperative form *conplectite* -- second-person plural present of *complecti* (to embrace). It was saved not as a literary monument but as a grammar example.\n\nLine 1: *Quin ergo, quando conuenit, conplectite?* The particle *quin* with a direct imperative is an urgent colloquial construction -- \"come on then,\" \"why not go ahead and.\" *Quando conuenit* means \"when it is fitting\" -- someone is prompting two characters to greet each other, as though watching from outside the scene.\n\nLine 2: The greeting is the joke. One character says *mi frater, salueto* (\"my brother, hello\"), using the archaic salutation form *salueto* (more old-fashioned than simple *salue*). The other responds *o soror, salue, mea* (\"oh my sister, hello\"). One says \"brother\"; the other says \"sister.\" This asymmetry is the comic kernel. In Atellan comedy, gender confusion points almost certainly to a disguise scenario -- *Maccus Virgo* is an entire play -- or to the kind of willful absurdity that the genre treats as a feature rather than a bug.\n\nLine 3: The comedy lands back in the workshop: *facite ut ignis feruat* (see to it that the fire boils), *ligna insipite* (feed in the wood), *far concidite* (chop up the grain). Three imperatives in rapid sequence, no explanatory framing. The spelt grain is surprising for a fulling workshop -- it is food, not part of the process -- suggesting the scene may take place partly in a kitchen, or that Pomponius is simply enjoying the incongruity. The line ends and the scene continues somewhere we cannot follow.\n\n---\n\n### Quintus Novius -- *Maccus Exul* (Maccus the Exile)\n\n**Latin text** *(Novius, Maccus Exul, fr. 48-50 Ribbeck, preserved by Nonius Marcellus 4M)*\n\n*Source note: Neither The Latin Library nor Splash Latino has a dedicated Novius page. The text is drawn from PHI Latin Texts (phi0592.phi001), which uses the Ribbeck edition as its base text. The ancient source is Nonius Marcellus, who preserves these lines under his entry for the demonstrative particle* em *in* De Compendiosa Doctrina.\n\n> Em: díxin itúrum hominem in Túscos tolútim?\n> Límen superum, quód mei misero saépe confregít caput,\n> Ínferum autem, dígitos omnis úbi ego diffregí meos.\n\n**Italian translation** *(original)*\n\n> Eccolo! Non l'avevo detto che quell'uomo se ne sarebbe andato in Toscana di gran carriera?\n> La soglia di sopra, che mi ha cosi' spesso sfondato la testa, povero me,\n> e quella di sotto, dove mi sono rotto tutte le dita dei piedi.\n\n**English translation** *(original)*\n\n> There! Did I not say that man would be off to Tuscany at a gallop?\n> The top threshold, which has so often smashed this wretch's head in,\n> and the bottom one, where I have broken all my toes.\n\n**Analysis**\n\nThe play is *Maccus Exul* -- Maccus the Exile. The title suggests a scenario in which Maccus has been driven out of somewhere, presumably following the kind of comprehensive disaster that defines his character. Exile -- from home, city, or household -- was a standard comic starting point, placing the fool in an unfamiliar situation with no safety net.\n\nLine 48: *Em: díxin itúrum hominem in Túscos tolútim?* The fragment opens with the demonstrative particle *em* -- \"there!\" or \"look!\" -- which is what Nonius Marcellus was citing it to illustrate. *Dixin* is a syncopated form of *dixine* (= *dixistine*, \"did I not say...?\"), a colloquial contraction found elsewhere in Plautus. *Tolutim* means moving at a trot or gallop, borrowed from the vocabulary of horse-gaits and used here to describe a rapid and undignified departure. Tuscany -- Etruria -- carried associations of provincial foreignness in the Roman comic imagination. Whether the destination is funny in itself, or whether the speed of the departure is the joke, depends on context we no longer have.\n\nLines 49-50: The scene shifts to a monologue of physical complaint. *Limen superum* is the top threshold -- the lintel above a doorway, at head height for a tall man and slightly above it for anyone moving at speed. *Limen inferum* is the raised stone at the base of the doorframe, where feet trip. Maccus's relationship with this doorway is characterized by one word: *saepe* -- often. He has often hit his head on the top threshold. He has broken all his toes on the bottom one. This is not a one-time accident; it is a pattern. The comedy is precisely in the *saepe*: Maccus is a man who, after multiple encounters with the same doorway, has still not learned to duck or step carefully. The exaggerated mask of the fool, with its large features, would have made the image immediately visible to any audience familiar with the character.\n\nThe fragment was preserved not for the image or the joke but because Nonius needed a citation for *em*. Maccus's chronic battle with his own doorframe survived two thousand years as a footnote to a particle. That is Atellan comedy's version of literary immortality.\n\n---\n\n## Final Rating\n\n**Tier: NC -- Not Comparable (both).**\n\nThe fragments of Pomponius and Novius together amount to roughly three hundred lines distributed across more than one hundred identified play titles -- an average of two to three lines per play. For Novius specifically, the surviving material is even sparser than Pomponius's: approximately one hundred lines across forty-three titles, averaging barely two lines per play. No complete scene survives for either author. The voice is identifiable in both cases -- colloquial, rustic, punchy, occasionally obscene -- and the per-line difficulty is assessable (roughly C to low B in intrinsic terms). But reliable multi-criteria rating requires connected text, and connected text is precisely what does not exist. Both are rated NC.\n\nAs a closing note: the genre they invented proved more durable than either of them. The same basic structure, the same characters, and many of the same jokes resurfaced in the *commedia dell'arte* fifteen centuries later. Maccus's mask and his tendency to collide with every doorframe he encountered did not die with the Roman Empire -- they just changed their name to Pulcinella.\n\n";
